const EXPLANATIONS = {
"1": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Otitis media with effusion commonly manifests as a sequela of an upper respiratory tract infection or acute otitis media with evidence of a middle ear effusion and the absence of middle ear inflammatory findings. Dysfunction of the eustachian tube is the underlying cause. Most cases are self-limited.</p><p>Otitis media with effusion (OME) is suggested by the presence of a middle ear effusion in the absence of signs of acute otitis media. Eustachian tube obstruction or dysfunction leads to the accumulation of serous fluid within the middle ear. Such dysfunction is common in the setting of an upper respiratory tract infection due to the inflammation of the nasal mucosa occluding the ostium of the eustachian tube, or as a sequela following acute otitis media. Fluid accumulation typically presents with hearing loss, ear tugging, or vertigo. Patients with OME lack findings of fever, tympanic membrane erythema, or retrotympanic pus, which are commonly found in acute otitis media. Treatment is supportive, as the condition is usually self-limited.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Adenoidectomy (Choice A) may be used in combination with tympanostomy for children with severe or persistent cases of OME. Surgical intervention is not indicated for this patient who presents with OME for several days, which will most likely resolve spontaneously.</p><p>Amoxicillin therapy (Choice B) and cefdinir therapy (Choice C) are unhelpful in the setting of OME, which occurs due to eustachian tube dysfunction rather than active bacterial infection.</p><p>Prednisone therapy (Choice D) is not recommended for most cases of OME because of the lack of demonstrated long-term benefit and the typically self-limiting nature of the disease.</p>",
"2": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Prader-Willi syndrome is a genetic disorder characterized by hypotonia, intellectual disabilities, behavioral issues, hyperphagia, and obesity. It is caused by absent expression of paternal genes in the chromosome region 15q11-13 and is a well-known example of a disorder associated with genetic imprinting and uniparental disomy.</p><p>Prader-Willi syndrome is a rare genetic disease caused by the lack of expression of paternal genes in the chromosome region 15q11-13. It is inherited in a sporadic pattern and may result from mutations or deletions in the paternal alleles, errors in the genetic imprinting of these alleles, or their complete absence in the case of maternal uniparental disomy. The syndrome has several characteristic features. Hypotonia is common and can lead to abnormal uterine position of the fetus and complicated labor, as well as feeding difficulties and failure to thrive in infancy. Developmental delays, intellectual disability, and disruptive behavioral patterns are commonly observed. It is associated with growth hormone deficiency and short stature. Hyperphagia and obesity are also hallmark manifestations of Prader-Willi syndrome. Individuals tend to have distinct facies with almond-shaped eyes, strabismus, and a wide, flattened nasal bridge. The prognosis is good, as patients tend to do well into adulthood.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, E, and F.</b></p><p>Angelman syndrome (Choice A) is a similar genetic disorder caused by loss of expression of the maternal gene UBE3A, which is also found in the chromosome region 15q11-13. The key features that distinguish it from Prader-Willi syndrome include hyperactivity, excitability, uncontrolled laughter, sleep disorders, and puppet-like ataxia (hand-flapping is characteristic).</p><p>Down syndrome (Choice B) results from trisomy of chromosome 21 and presents with characteristic physical features including broad, flat, facial features with prominent epicanthal folds, and a single palmar crease. Patients may have obesity, though the syndrome is not generally like Prader-Willi syndrome.</p><p>Fetal alcohol syndrome (Choice C) is associated with intellectual disability, developmental delay, and physical malformations such as microcephaly, facial dysmorphia, limb deformities, and congenital heart disease, as well as a history of maternal alcohol use during pregnancy.</p><p>Rett disorder (Choice E) results from sporadic mutations in the MECP2 gene on the X chromosome and affects those assigned female at birth. Symptoms typically appear in the first 18 months of life, with progressive motor impairments, developmental delays, and significant scoliosis and kyphosis.</p><p>Williams syndrome (Choice F) is characterized by distinct elf-like facies, verbal and social skills that often mask intellectual disabilities, and an extremely friendly demeanor. Supravalvular aortic stenosis is common. It results from a microdeletion on chromosome 7 that includes the elastin gene.</p>",
"3": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Apgar scoring is performed to assess the newborn at 1 and 5 minutes after birth. Newborns with lower Apgar scores may require further evaluation and/or intervention.</p><p>Apgar scoring is performed at 1 and 5 minutes after birth to assess the status of the newborn and need for possible resuscitation. The Apgar score consists of points given in five different categories (appearance, pulse, grimace or reflex irritability, activity/muscle tone, and respiratory effort) summed together to compute the Apgar score. Each category is given 0, 1, or 2 points, with 0 points for absent function, 1 for diminished or irregular function, and 2 for a normal, expected presentation. Newborns with lower scores, especially below 7, may require further evaluation and/or intervention. This patient would receive a score of 1 for peripheral cyanosis, 1 for decreased muscle tone, 1 for decreased reflex activity, 1 for pulse less than 100/min, and 1 for irregular respirations, totaling an Apgar score of 5. This indicates the potential need for further resuscitation. Apgar scores are not necessarily associated with later functional or developmental abnormalities.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</b></p><p>An Apgar score of 0-1 (Choice A) or 2-3 (Choice B) would require extreme deficiency in several assessed categories (absent pulse, general cyanosis, absent muscle tone, absent respirations, and/or no response to stimulation). This patient has objective, scorable neonatal Apgar features, even if they are suboptimal.</p><p>An Apgar score of 7-8 (Choice D) or 9-10 (Choice E) would be more reassuring but would require the newborn to have a pink acyanotic appearance, pulse greater than 100/min, active response to stimulation, normal muscle tone with active movement, or a vigorous cry.</p>",
"4": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Patients with a bicuspid aortic valve, or another structural abnormality of the cardiac valves, are at much greater risk for infective endocarditis than the general population.</p><p>Bicuspid aortic valves may be inherited or sporadic. As a result of their geometry and abnormal leaflet excursion, they are subject to more blood flow turbulence (generating the murmur) and shear forces. This leads to increased local tissue inflammation and calcification compared with trileaflet valves. Bicuspid valves are more prone to the development of early aortic stenosis or aortic regurgitation. Abnormal valve architecture can also present a nidus for infection; hence, patients are at increased risk for bacterial endocarditis. There is also a small risk for systemic thromboembolic disease from clots that form on the valve. Congenital and hereditary conditions associated with bicuspid aortic valves include Turner syndrome, Marfan syndrome, Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, coarctation of the aorta, patent ductus arteriosus, and ventricular septal defects.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Brain abscess (Choice B) is a potential complication of infective endocarditis due to septic emboli. A bicuspid aortic valve is not a direct risk factor for brain abscess.</p><p>Congestive heart failure (Choice C) is a long-term complication of an untreated bicuspid aortic valve that may result due to chronically increased left ventricle afterload or severe valvular disease, which can manifest as aortic stenosis or regurgitation. Patients with bicuspid valves should be monitored regularly to allow for prompt surgical intervention if needed to prevent progressive heart failure.</p><p>Dissecting aortic aneurysm (Choice D) occurs more frequently in patients with bicuspid aortic valves compared with the general population. A large percentage of patients develop an enlarged aorta susceptible to aneurysm formation. Aneurysm dissection is a less common complication than infective endocarditis.</p><p>Syncope (Choice E) may result from one of the conditions associated with a bicuspid aortic valve, such as advanced heart failure, but is not a directly correlated risk.</p>",
"5": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Cyclic neutropenia is a rare inherited immunodeficiency syndrome associated with dysfunctional neutrophil elastase and characterized by periodic episodes of neutropenia and infection. Most cases follow a cyclic pattern that reoccurs every 21 days and lasts for 3 to 6 days.</p><p>Cyclic neutropenia is a rare inherited immunodeficiency syndrome characterized by periodic episodes of neutropenia and infection. Most cases follow a cyclic pattern that reoccurs every 21 days and typically lasts for 3 to 6 days. Classic symptoms include fever, malaise, and ulcers affecting the oral mucous membranes. Some patients may also develop pharyngitis and chronic periodontal disease that can result in early tooth loss. During episodes of neutropenia, patients are at increased risk for bacterial infections that may involve the respiratory tract, gastrointestinal tract, urinary tract, or skin. The pathophysiology is not well understood, however, the disease is associated with mutations in the gene encoding neutrophil elastase. The diagnosis can be confirmed by monitoring a patient's neutrophil count several times weekly for at least 6 weeks. Genetic testing for mutations in the neutrophil elastase gene is also recommended. Acute management involves treatment of the current infection with appropriate therapies and maintaining good oral/dental hygiene. Long-term therapy with human granulocyte-colony stimulating factor is effective in keeping neutrophils at normal concentrations in the blood.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, E, F, G, H, and I.</b></p><p>Chédiak-Higashi syndrome (Choice A) is caused by mutations in the lysosomal trafficking regulator gene (LYST) essential for normal formation and transportation of lysosomes within the cell. It presents with frequent bacterial infections, oculocutaneous albinism, peripheral neuropathy, and progressive neurologic dysfunction.</p><p>Dyskeratosis congenita (Choice C) is a rare genetic condition associated with bone marrow failure. It is characterized by a triad of skin abnormalities (such as a reticular hyperpigmentation pattern, pale-gray macules, areas of atrophy), dystrophic nails, and oral leukoplakia. Patients will have pancytopenia.</p><p>Fanconi syndrome (Choice D) can be an inherited or acquired condition that causes malabsorption of solutes in the renal proximal tubule. Fanconi anemia, however, is a separate genetic condition that causes bone marrow suppression, pancytopenia, and recurrent infections. It is also characterized by short stature, microcephaly, developmental delay, and café-au-lait skin lesions.</p><p>Hypersplenism (Choice E) can have multiple causes, but commonly involves splenomegaly and cytopenias of all three cell lines (anemia, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia) to varying degrees as a result of sequestration of circulating cells and platelets. This patient's periodic episodes and isolated neutropenia are more consistent with cyclic neutropenia.</p><p>Leukemia (Choice F), especially acute lymphoblastic leukemia, is a common pediatric malignancy of B- or T-lymphocytes that may present with malaise, fatigue, weight loss, splenomegaly, lymphadenopathy, anemia, and thrombocytopenia, with circulating leukemic blasts on peripheral blood smear.</p><p>Severe congenital neutropenia (SCN) (Choice G) is another congenital disorder characterized by low concentrations of circulating neutrophils and recurrent bacterial infections. In comparison to cyclic neutropenia, SCN is typically more severe and presents with infections in the first few months of life, and has a high risk for transformation into acute myeloid leukemia.</p><p>Viral illness (Choice H), especially with parvovirus B19, Epstein-Barr virus, cytomegalovirus, HIV, and dengue, can cause bone marrow suppression with cytopenias of varying degrees. These illnesses often present with additional characteristic symptoms and do not follow a cyclic pattern.</p><p>Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) deficiency (Choice I) results in megaloblastic anemia, which presents with macrocytosis and segmented neutrophils. It is more common in patients who chronically abuse alcohol, or in patients with a Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, Crohn disease, or malabsorptive disorders.</p>",
"6": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Rocky Mountain spotted fever is a tick-borne illness, associated with fever, headache, and petechial rash that starts days to weeks following a bite from an infected tick. Patients may develop life-threatening complications from infection including encephalitis, acute respiratory distress syndrome, cardiac arrhythmias, and complications caused by coagulopathy. Diagnosis is made by immunoglobulin testing. Patients rapidly respond to treatment with tetracyclines, such as doxycycline.</p><p>Rocky Mountain spotted fever is a tick-borne disease caused by Rickettsia rickettsii, which are gram-negative, obligate intracellular organisms. These organisms target endothelial cells, triggering vascular injury and permeability. This illness is most common in the early spring and summer, following a tick bite in an endemic area (eg, Mid-Atlantic/Southeastern United States). Incubation occurs 2 to 14 days after exposure and manifestations include fever, headache, and petechial rash. Other manifestations include conjunctivitis, seizures, encephalitis, acute respiratory distress syndrome, cardiac arrhythmias, and coagulopathy. Laboratory testing shows thrombocytopenia, hyponatremia, increased liver enzymes, increased partial thromboplastin, and prothrombin times. If there are concerns of central nervous system involvement, lumbar puncture is recommended. Serologic testing for IgM and IgG antibodies against Rickettsia is the most used test, and patients are most often treated with doxycycline therapy. In general, patients have a rapid response to therapy. Empiric treatment should be initiated when clinical suspicion is high, as early treatment improves the prognosis, and laboratory assays are not perfectly sensitive or specific.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, E, F, G, and I.</b></p><p>Dengue fever (Choice A) presents with febrile illness, with associated headache, retro-orbital pain, and marked bone and muscle pains. Symptoms last approximately 1 week and patients may develop hemorrhagic complications. Dengue is transmitted by Aedes aegypti mosquitoes in tropical and subtropical climates. This patient's presentation is not consistent with dengue fever.</p><p>Erysipelas (Choice B) is a superficial skin infection with fiery-red color and ridge-like, sharp borders commonly seen on the face or lower extremity. The most common causative organism is group A streptococcus and patients are treated with antibiotics.</p><p>Erythema infectiosum (fifth disease) (Choice C) is associated with parvovirus B19 infection. This presents with pink patches on the bilateral cheeks of children giving an appearance of slapped cheeks. This facial rash may be followed by a lacy, macular eruption on the trunk and extremities. However, it typically affects school-aged children who are often otherwise asymptomatic.</p><p>Exanthema subitum (Choice D), or roseola infantum, is caused by human herpesvirus 6 infection and associated with several days of fever, sometimes accompanied by seizures. As the fevers subside, a characteristic, blanching, macular eruption forms on the neck and trunk which then spreads to the face and extremities. The characteristic high fever followed by a rash helps to identify roseola infantum as the cause. Treatment is supportive. In most cases, it is a benign condition with a limited course.</p><p>Measles (Choice E) is caused by the highly contagious paramyxovirus. It is more common in children than adults. It characteristically presents with prodromal fever, cough, coryza, conjunctivitis, and a confluent maculopapular rash that starts at the head or neck and spreads to the trunk, excluding the palms and soles.</p><p>Meningococcemia (Choice F) is caused by Neisseria meningitidis and is a common cause of bacterial meningitis. Clinical manifestations may include fever, nausea, vomiting, myalgias, and headache, which may be followed by neck stiffness and altered mental status. Patients may develop an initial petechial or purpuric rash, suggestive of bloodstream involvement, which may progress to complications that include disseminated intravascular coagulation and Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome (purpura fulminans).</p><p>Mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome (Kawasaki disease) (Choice G) is a multisystemic vasculitic disease in children characterized by fever for at least 5 days, bilateral nonexudative conjunctivitis, a polymorphous rash, lymphadenopathy, erythema and edema of the hands and feet, and erythema affecting the mucous membranes with dry, cracked lips. Complications include coronary artery aneurysm and patients are managed with intravenous immunoglobulin and aspirin therapy.</p><p>Typhoid fever (Choice I) is associated with bacterial infection with Salmonella Typhi. In endemic settings, it is most common in children and presents with pain, fever, and chills, along with diarrhea. Diagnosis is made with bacterial cultures and patients are treated with antibiotics.</p>",
"7": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Global developmental delay is defined as having significant delay in two or more domains of development. Four-year-old children should be able to hop on one foot, copy a square and simple stick figure, speak in sentences, and play cooperatively with friends.</p><p>Screening for developmental milestone achievement is an important element of the pediatric well-child examination. Gross motor, fine motor, cognitive, social, and language milestones should be evaluated. This patient has a presentation consistent with global developmental delay, defined as significant delay in two or more domains of development. By age 4 years, most children can draw a stick figure, speak in full sentences that can be understood by others, hop on one foot, and play cooperatively with friends. Patients who are delayed in meeting developmental milestones should be evaluated for underlying causes and referred for early childhood intervention where they have access to speech therapists, physical therapists, and occupational therapists who can work with them.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, E, F, G, and H.</b></p><p>Choices B, C, D, E, F, G, and H are incorrect. Gross motor milestones for a 4-year-old include hopping on one foot and throwing a ball overhand. Fine motor skills include drawing a square and a simple stick figure. Social milestones include playing cooperatively with friends and having imaginary friends. Language milestones include telling complex stories, identifying colors and numbers, and speaking in sentences.</p>",
"8": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Congenital cytomegalovirus infection presents with intrauterine growth restriction, microcephaly, intracranial calcifications, hearing loss, seizures, hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, thrombocytopenia, and petechial rash. A primary maternal infection has a high risk for vertical transmission via the placenta to the fetus. Intracranial calcifications have a characteristic periventricular distribution and may be detected by ultrasonography or CT scan.</p><p>This newborn presents with findings consistent with congenital cytomegalovirus (CMV) infection. CMV, also known as human herpesvirus 5, belongs to the family of herpesviruses, and possesses a large, double-stranded DNA genome. Pregnant women most often contract CMV through exposure from young children, often through daycare centers. A primary maternal infection has a high risk for vertical transmission via the placenta to the fetus. Congenital CMV infection presents with intrauterine growth restriction, microcephaly, intracranial calcifications, hearing loss, seizures, hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, thrombocytopenia, and petechial rash. Congenital CMV infection is the most common cause of nonhereditary sensorineural hearing loss. In about 10% of cases, hearing loss may be an isolated finding without other symptoms of infection present. Hearing loss is also the most common sequela of an initially asymptomatic newborn. Fetal hydrops is a potential complication of CMV infection, although less common than in the setting of congenital parvovirus B19 infection. Intracranial calcifications have a characteristic periventricular distribution and may be detected by ultrasonography or CT scan. The diagnosis is confirmed with viral culture or polymerase chain amplification from urine or blood within the first week of life. Treatment is with antiviral agents such as ganciclovir or valganciclovir.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, E, F, and G.</b></p><p>Listeria monocytogenes (Choice B) is a gram-positive, facultative anaerobic bacillus that may cause neonatal listeriosis via the transplacental route or through ascending vaginal infection with chorioamnionitis, frequently resulting in spontaneous abortion or stillbirth. Newborns may present with signs and symptoms of sepsis, meningitis, or pneumonia, as well as a characteristic rash known as granulomatosis infantiseptica, which consists of diffuse, pale, granulomatous cutaneous nodules.</p><p>Parvovirus B19 (Choice C) infection typically occurs in the second or third trimester and presents with fetal anemia caused by viral tropism for bone marrow and infection of erythroid progenitor cells. Fetal and neonatal anemia may result in tachycardia, hydrops, high-output heart failure, and death.</p><p>Rubella (Choice D) is a virus and the causative agent of congenital rubella. Congenital rubella infection exhibits a classic triad of cataracts, sensorineural deafness, and congenital heart disease. Newborns also typically display a diffuse purpuric rash. Congenital rubella syndrome is notable as one of the few congenital syndromes that presents with both cataracts and deafness.</p><p>Streptococcus agalactiae (group B) (Choice E) is an important cause of neonatal sepsis and is typically acquired from the vaginal canal during delivery. It does not typically result in intracranial calcifications or sensorineural deafness.</p><p>S. intermedius (Choice F) is a member of Streptococcus anginosus group (SAG). SAG organisms exhibit a tendency to form abscesses, including hepatic and intracranial abscesses. However, they are not a common cause of congenital or neonatal infection and do not present with intracranial calcifications.</p><p>Toxoplasma gondii (Choice G) is an apicomplexan that may be acquired through transplacental infection. Congenital toxoplasmosis presents with the classic triad of chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus, and intracranial calcifications, which have a characteristic ring-enhancing appearance. The absence of hydrocephalus and the presence of periventricular calcifications is more consistent with congenital CMV infection.</p>",
"9": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Tetralogy of Fallot is a congenital heart malformation characterized by right ventricular outflow tract (RVOT) obstruction, ventricular septal defect, an overriding aorta, and right ventricular hypertrophy. Patients present with “tet” spells that occur as a result of transient obstruction of the RVOT leading to cyanosis. Treatment is with surgical correction of the underlying anatomic defects.</p><p>Tetralogy of Fallot is the most likely diagnosis in this patient with paroxysmal cyanosis during crying spells and a 3/6 systolic ejection murmur on examination. Tetralogy of Fallot consists of pulmonary infundibular stenosis, overriding aorta, ventricular septal defect (VSD), and right ventricular hypertrophy, resulting in a right-to-left shunt with cyanosis first noted in infancy. Right ventricular outflow tract (RVOT) obstruction eventually leads to right ventricular hypertrophy. The murmur heard with Tetralogy of Fallot, which is typically a holosystolic murmur best heard in the left lower sternal border, is caused by RVOT obstruction, not as often by the VSD. Paroxysmal cyanosis that occurs during periods of crying is a classic presentation and these episodes are referred to as “tet” spells. Patients during one of these spells often exhibit rapid, deep breathing, agitation, and progressive cyanosis. These periods of cyanosis occur as a result of transient obstruction or occlusion of the RVOT leading to decreased blood flow through the pulmonary circulation, although the cause of the underlying transient obstruction is unclear. Treatment of tetralogy of Fallot is with surgical correction. Surgery aims to relieve the RVOT obstruction, close the VSD, and separate the pulmonary and systemic circulation. Surgery is usually performed in the first 6 months of life and almost always within the first year.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Atrial septal defect (Choice A) is a common congenital malformation of the interatrial septum that results in a left-to-right shunt between the atria. Mixing of oxygenated blood from the left atrium with deoxygenated blood returning from the superior vena cava results in higher oxygen concentration in the right atrium and ventricle than normal. It does not cause cyanosis. It is associated with a fixed split-second heart sound on cardiac auscultation.</p><p>Coarctation of the aorta (Choice B) often presents in childhood with hypertension in an upper extremity and a systolic murmur. It is associated with weak lower extremity pulses and differentially lower blood pressure in the lower extremities.</p><p>Isolated pulmonary stenosis (Choice C) is typically a benign, congenital heart defect when found in children and not associated with another congenital heart syndrome, such as tetralogy of Fallot or Noonan syndrome.</p><p>Ventricular septal defect (Choice E) refers to a defect in the interventricular septum, most commonly the membranous portion. It is characterized by a holosystolic murmur best heard in the left lower sternal border. It does not cause cyanosis.</p>",
"10": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: NADPH is produced from NADP+ by the enzyme glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) and is required for the reduction of glutathione, which helps scavenge reactive oxygen species in erythrocytes. The absence of G6PD results in increased oxidative stress that can lead to hemolysis when patients are exposed to certain medications such as trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole and other sulfa drugs.</p><p>Neonatal jaundice is defined as an increased bilirubin (&gt;5 mg/dL) that results from either increased hemolysis (bilirubin generation) or decreased bilirubin conjugation or excretion. Hyperbilirubinemia can be divided into direct and indirect forms in which either conjugated or unconjugated bilirubin accumulate, respectively. It can also be divided into physiologic and pathologic forms. This patient has anemia and an indirect (unconjugated) hyperbilirubinemia that has presented in the first few days of life, suggesting a pathologic hemolytic process. The differential includes immune-mediated hemolysis, inherited erythrocyte membrane defects, and erythrocyte enzyme deficiencies. For this patient with a negative Coombs test and Mediterranean background, the most likely cause of his unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia is deficiency of the enzyme glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD). G6PD is an enzyme in erythrocytes that makes NADPH from NADP+. An inadequate supply of NADPH leads to decreased concentrations of glutathione, which is a critical scavenger of reactive oxygen species within erythrocytes. Increased oxidative stress, which can occur with exposure to sulfa-containing compounds, results in hemolysis.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Dehydration (Choice A) can result in jaundice in the newborn due to increased enterohepatic circulation, renal resorption, and concentration of bilirubin in the serum. This patient's findings are more consistent with a hemolytic process.</p><p>Milk protein allergy (Choice C) may occur in infants fed with both cow's milk formula and breast milk in the first year of life. Patients may present with IgE-mediated manifestations including anaphylaxis, urticaria, and angioedema, or with non-IgE-mediated symptoms of reflux, enterocolitis, or enteropathy.</p><p>Physiologic jaundice (Choice D) occurs in nearly all newborns and is caused by the increased breakdown of red blood cells, immature liver metabolism of bilirubin, and increased enterohepatic circulation of bilirubin. Features include development after 24 hours of life, a rate of bilirubin increase of less than 0.5 mg/dL daily, a maximum peak of 14 to 15 mg/dL, and a predominance of indirect bilirubin (&gt;90%). In this patient, the increased bilirubin concentration to 20 mg/dL and low hematocrit should prompt evaluation for pathologic causes.</p><p>Sepsis (Choice E) in the newborn can cause indirect hyperbilirubinemia. Neonatal sepsis may manifest as a range from asymptomatic to hemodynamic collapse. Early symptoms include irritability, lethargy, and poor feeding. This patient should also be evaluated for infection, but the most likely explanation is G6PD deficiency.</p>",
"11": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Osteomyelitis may occur from hematogenous spread, spread of existing skin or soft tissue infection, or trauma. The most common organism associated with puncture wounds is Pseudomonas aeruginosa. X-ray or MRI are used to clinically evaluate patients with suspected osteomyelitis, although MRI is more sensitive. Treatment with debridement, operative cultures to identify the causative organism, and antibiotics are the gold standard for treating osteomyelitis.</p><p>Osteomyelitis may occur from hematogenous spreading from adjacent skin and soft tissue infection, or direct inoculation in the case of trauma. Osteomyelitis clinically presents with pain at the site, erythema, tenderness to palpation, swelling, decreased range of motion, and fever. Pseudomonas aeruginosa is an aerobic, oxidase positive, gram-negative bacteria that is associated with hospital acquired infections, as well as osteomyelitis following a puncture wound (classically through an athletic shoe). Patients who suffer puncture wounds and diabetic patients are at the highest risk for Pseudomonas-associated osteomyelitis. X-ray or MRI may be used to identify osteomyelitis, although MRI is more sensitive, and patients should undergo operative debridement to remove necrotic tissue, cultures to identify the organism, and be initiated on empiric antibiotic coverage.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Aeromonas hydrophila (Choice A) is a gram-negative anaerobic bacteria present in water. These organisms may cause diarrhea, opportunistic infections in immunocompromised individuals, and wound infections in patients in contact with contaminated fresh water or soil at the time of injury.</p><p>Group A streptococcus (Choice B), or Streptococcus pyogenes, is a gram-positive bacterium that may cause pharyngitis, skin infections such as impetigo, erysipelas, or cellulitis, and life-threatening infections including necrotizing fasciitis or toxic shock syndrome.</p><p>Klebsiella pneumoniae (Choice C) is a gram-negative bacillus that may cause pneumonia in patients who are at risk for aspiration pneumonia, such as those with alcohol use disorder. It is a less likely cause of cellulitis or osteomyelitis than Pseudomonas in this case.</p><p>Staphylococcus aureus (Choice E) is the most common cause of osteomyelitis. This may be related to direct inoculation or hematogenous spread, such as in the case of sepsis or intravenous drug use. Pseudomonas osteomyelitis is more common in the setting of puncture wounds.</p>",
"12": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Androgen insensitivity syndrome occurs because of a defect in the androgen receptor and results in persons with genotypically male chromosomes developing phenotypically female characteristics. Diagnosis is made with karyotype analysis.</p><p>Androgen insensitivity syndrome is caused by a defect in the androgen receptor complex resulting in a genotypic 46,XY person developing phenotypic female or atypical genitalia and female secondary sexual characteristics. Testes are present and produce testosterone normally, but the absence of a functioning androgen receptor prevents hormone binding and thereby prevents the development of secondary male sexual characteristics. Patients present with female external genitalia, scant pubic and axillary hair, absent uterus and fallopian tubes, and a rudimentary vagina. Menses does not occur because of the lack of cyclic progesterone and estrogen, and the lack of a functional uterus with endometrial lining. Patients show increased concentrations of testosterone, estrogen, and luteinizing hormone on laboratory evaluation. Diagnosis is made with karyotype analysis showing XY chromosomes in a patient with phenotypically female characteristics.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>A follow-up examination in 6 months (Choice A) would be inappropriate as early recognition and diagnosis of androgen insensitivity syndrome promotes clinical intervention where possible. Additionally, evaluation for causes of primary amenorrhea is generally indicated if menarche has not occurred at or before age 16 years.</p><p>While measurement of serum estrogen concentration (Choice C) will show an increased concentration of estrogen, it would not show the specific diagnosis of androgen insensitivity syndrome, which is diagnosed via karyotype analysis.</p><p>An MRI of the pituitary gland (Choice D) can identify a pituitary tumor. A pituitary tumor is associated with gynecomastia, galactorrhea, and amenorrhea when prolactin is functionally produced by the tumor. Other signs of a pituitary tumor include features related to its mass effect in the sella turcica with loss of other pituitary hormones, or damage to the optic chiasm resulting in bitemporal hemianopsia on examination.</p><p>Estrogen therapy (Choice E) would be inappropriate, as patients with androgen insensitivity syndrome show increased concentrations of estrogen.</p>",
"13": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Lupus nephritis is a common complication of systemic lupus erythematosus and may present with oliguria or anuria, hypertension, uremia, hyperkalemia, and hypervolemia. Laboratory studies show hypocomplementemia with decreased serum concentrations of early classical pathway components such as C1q, C2, C3, C4, and CH50, as well as increased creatinine.</p><p>Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is an autoimmune disorder with a wide range of presentations. Cutaneous manifestations include an erythematous rash over the malar cheeks that spares the nasolabial folds after sun exposure or discoid lesions with hyperpigmented edges and pink, atrophic centers. Painless oral ulcers and nonspecific hair loss may also occur. Fatigue, joint pain, and fever are also common presenting signs. Lupus nephritis is a common complication of SLE, particularly in younger women. Patients with lupus nephritis typically present with evidence of active SLE and may additionally display oliguria or anuria, signs and symptoms of hypertension (eg, headache, visual disturbance), uremia (eg, fatigue, nausea, vomiting, pruritus, altered mental status), hyperkalemia (eg, peaked T waves), and hypervolemia with peripheral edema. Lupus nephritis involves glomerular deposition of antibodies, including IgG, IgM, and IgA, as well as activation of the classical complement pathway. Laboratory studies therefore generally show hypocomplementemia with decreased serum concentrations of early classical pathway components such as C1q, C2, C3, C4, and CH50, as well as increased creatinine. Kidney biopsy is useful for disease staging and may show a range of histologic patterns from mesangial glomerulonephritis to diffuse proliferative or membranous glomerulonephritis.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Eosinophil count (Choice A) is typically normal in patients with lupus nephritis. Increased eosinophil concentrations may be observed in the setting of acute interstitial nephritis.</p><p>Serum C9 concentration (Choice E), as well as other late complement components, are typically normal in patients with SLE and lupus nephritis.</p><p>Factor D (Choice B) and Factor P (Choice C) are components of the alternative complement pathway, which is not antibody dependent. The alternative complement pathway is typically not dysregulated in the setting of SLE, and serum concentrations of these components are normal.</p>",
"14": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Asthma is characterized by the reversible obstruction to airflow caused by spasm of smooth muscle in the bronchi and small airways. It is often triggered by an allergen (eg, smoke, pollen), exercise, or temperature change. In the case of allergen-induced asthma, bronchoconstriction is triggered by IgE-mediated activation of mast cells, followed by degranulation and histamine release. This mast cell activation is classified as a type I hypersensitivity reaction. Treatment involves mast cell stabilizers and anti-IgE antibodies, among many other agents.</p><p>This patient's presenting signs and symptoms of exertional-induced wheezing, chest tightness, dyspnea, and tachypnea are suggestive of an asthma exacerbation. Asthma is characterized by the reversible obstruction to airflow caused by spasm of smooth muscle in the bronchi and small airways. It is often triggered by an allergen (eg, smoke, pollen), exercise, or temperature change. In the case of allergen-induced asthma, bronchoconstriction is triggered by IgE-mediated activation of mast cells, followed by degranulation and histamine release. Such mast cell activation is classified as a type I hypersensitivity reaction. These reactions are composed of two phases, the immediate and the late phase. In the immediate phase, preformed IgE on the surface of mast cells and basophils is crosslinked by an antigen leading to their degranulation and release of histamine, which results in vasodilation, vascular permeability, and bronchoconstriction among other effects. The late phase occurs hours after the initial insult and is mediated by the release of prostaglandins and leukotrienes synthesized by the mast cells. Treatment for asthma depends on the classification (intermittent, mild/moderate/severe persistent). In the case of exacerbations triggered by allergens, agents that act on mast cells or IgE may be employed. The prototypical mast cell stabilizer is cromolyn sodium, which prevents mast cell degranulation and the release of histamine and leukotrienes. It is used in the treatment of asthma and exercise-induced bronchospasm. Another agent is omalizumab, a monoclonal antibody against IgE used to treat patients with asthma and increased IgE concentrations.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, E, and F.</b></p><p>Activation of alveolar macrophages (Choice A) is seen in many pulmonary diseases. Alveolar macrophages are the first line of the innate immune system in defending against inhaled pathogens. There are multiple cytokine-based and surface receptor-based mechanisms that activate alveolar macrophages. While any infectious, irritant, or inflammatory stimulus can result in an exacerbation of concomitant asthma, this occurs secondary to bronchoconstriction or airway narrowing as opposed to macrophage activation, which does not directly trigger this process.</p><p>Similarly, increased neutrophil-endothelial cell adhesion (Choice C) is a cytokine and surface molecule—mediated process (eg, intercellular adhesion molecules) that constitutes one part of the inflammatory response. While it may trigger a concomitant asthma exacerbation, it is not the direct mechanism.</p><p>Increased pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure (Choice D) describes the pathophysiology of pulmonary edema. Increased hydrostatic pressure results in extravasation of fluid into the interstitial spaces of the lung, and finally, into the alveolar space impairing gas exchange.</p><p>Increased pulmonary vascular resistance (Choice E) describes the underlying pathophysiology of pulmonary hypertension. Increased pulmonary vascular tone, chronic hypoxia, venous thromboembolic disease, and left-sided heart failure are many mechanisms that increase pulmonary vascular resistance. It may be increased in asthma secondary to hypoxia but is not the primary mechanism of an asthma exacerbation.</p><p>Parenchymal infiltration by lymphocytes (Choice F) is seen in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) and is a mechanism for inflammation and destruction of small airways and alveolar stroma and parenchyma. In contrast to asthma, COPD is marked by irreversible obstructive airway disease. Like asthma, COPD exacerbations can also be triggered by many underlying causes, including infection, irritant inhalation, exertion, and weather changes. Treatment for acute exacerbations of the two diseases is similar, though there is not a consistent role for mast cell stabilizers in the management of COPD.</p>",
"15": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Patients with herpes simplex virus encephalitis may present with headache, lethargy, seizures, and fever. Cerebrospinal fluid findings include polymerase chain reaction test positive for HSV. Treatment is with intravenous acyclovir.</p><p>This newborn with seizures, lethargy, and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) with lymphocytosis and increased erythrocytes has a presentation concerning for encephalitis caused by herpes simplex virus (HSV). Neonatal infection with HSV can occur via the placenta or through direct inoculation as the newborn passes through the birth canal of a mother with active lesions. Infection with HSV can cause both cutaneous herpetic lesions and systemic manifestations such as meningoencephalitis. Patients may present with altered mental status, headache, fever, seizures, and focal neurologic deficits. In newborns, lethargy may be the primary presenting sign. Lumbar puncture should be performed in all patients with suspected HSV encephalitis, and often shows mildly increased protein, normal or mildly decreased glucose, increased erythrocytes, and increased leukocytes with a lymphocytic predominance. Definitive diagnosis requires polymerase chain reaction testing to detect HSV in the CSF. Treatment requires intravenous acyclovir.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Cytomegalovirus (Choice A) in a newborn manifests as hearing loss, seizures, a petechial rash, intracranial calcifications, organomegaly, and lymphadenopathy especially if contracted through vertical transmission prior to birth.</p><p>Escherichia coli (Choice B) and Group B streptococcus (Choice C) are both common causes of neonatal meningitis. Patients may present with lethargy and seizures, but the CSF would contain decreased glucose, increased protein, and a markedly increased leukocyte count (often &gt;1000/mm³) with a neutrophilic predominance.</p><p>Toxoplasma gondii (Choice E) can be transmitted from mother to fetus through the placenta. Patients with congenital toxoplasmosis often present with chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus, and intracranial calcifications.</p>",
"16": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objectives: Patients with moderate to severe dehydration may present with tachycardia, tachypnea, hypotension, delayed capillary refill, lethargy, sunken fontanelles, and decreased urine output, and require volume resuscitation with intravenous crystalloids.</p><p>This infant with a recent gastrointestinal illness has a presentation consistent with dehydration. Dehydration is categorized as mild, moderate, or severe, based on the presenting signs and symptoms. Patients with mild dehydration will present with normal vital signs as well as normal mental status and urine output. Patients with moderate to severe dehydration may present with tachycardia, tachypnea, hypotension, delayed capillary refill, lethargy, sunken fontanelles, and decreased urine output. Patients with moderate to severe dehydration should receive an initial bolus of 20 cc/kg of intravenous crystalloid (eg, normal saline, lactated ringers) for volume expansion. Patients with severe dehydration and hypovolemic shock can quickly decompensate and progress to cardiovascular collapse if not adequately resuscitated, and therefore, should be frequently re-evaluated with additional fluid boluses given if needed.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Oral rehydration (Choice A) is appropriate in cases of mild dehydration in a child that is tolerating oral intake. This patient, with vomiting, poor feeding, and signs of more significant dehydration, needs appropriate resuscitation with intravenous crystalloid.</p><p>Continuous infusion of fluids at maintenance doses (Choice B) would be appropriate only after adequate volume resuscitation and euvolemia has been achieved.</p><p>Intravenous administration of dopamine (Choice C) can be used in the treatment of cardiogenic shock, although norepinephrine or dobutamine are often preferred. Dopamine has been thought to maintain kidney perfusion because of vasodilation at low doses, increase cardiac output by increasing stroke volume at intermediate doses, and raise mean arterial pressure via increased vasoconstriction at higher doses. This phenomenon is not well demonstrated in high quality studies, and dosing variability plus arrhythmogenicity limits dopamine’s use in practice.</p><p>Intravenous administration of fresh frozen plasma (Choice D) contains clotting factors in addition to fibrinogen, antithrombin III, and other plasma proteins. It can be used to replace clotting factors in coagulopathic patients.</p>",
"17": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Acute poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis is a type of nephritic syndrome characterized by dark colored urine, hypertension, hematuria, and proteinuria following an infection with group A streptococcus. Immune complex deposition within the glomerulus and mesangium leads to decreased concentrations of serum C3.</p><p>Acute poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN) is a type of nephritic syndrome characterized by dark colored urine, hypertension, acute kidney injury, hematuria, and proteinuria following an infection with group A streptococcus. The pathogenesis of PSGN involves streptococcal antigens complexing with antibodies, deposition of immune complexes in the glomerular basement membrane, and triggering of complement activation and inflammation. Light microscopy shows deposits of C3 and IgG in the glomerulus and mesangium. The deposition of C3 in the glomerulus and mesangium leads to decreased concentrations of serum C3. Urinalysis shows hematuria and variable proteinuria, with or without red blood cell casts. Hypertension associated with PSGN is caused by salt and fluid retention resulting in volume overload. Fluid retention can lead to generalized edema including periorbital, pretibial, and pedal edema. It can also lead to complications involving heart failure, pulmonary edema, and respiratory distress.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Serum antinuclear antibody assay (Choice B) shows increased antinuclear antibodies in many autoimmune and inflammatory diseases such as rheumatoid arthritis and systemic lupus erythematosus and is neither sensitive nor specific for PSGN.</p><p>While urinalysis can be helpful in the diagnosis of acute PSGN, demonstrating hematuria and proteinuria, a urine culture (Choice C) would not be helpful. A urine culture can help in the diagnosis of urinary tract infections such as cystitis or pyelonephritis.</p><p>Echocardiography (Choice D) can evaluate for the presence of heart failure, which is another potential complication of streptococcal pharyngitis in the setting of rheumatic heart disease. This patient's lungs are clear to auscultation, indicating heart failure is less likely as a cause of her apparent hypervolemic state.</p><p>Renal ultrasonography (Choice E) can help with the diagnosis of diseases that alter the size, shape, or tissue of kidneys, such as cysts in autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease, tumors, abscesses, or urinary tract obstructions. Immune complex deposition in acute PSGN is a microscopic change seen on light microscopy, not ultrasonography of the kidneys.</p>",
"18": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome (Kawasaki disease) is a multisystemic vasculitis resulting in acute arteritis with a risk for the development of coronary artery aneurysms. Untreated disease can result in severe cardiac complications, including aneurysmal expansion, rupture, myocardial infarction, and congestive heart failure.</p><p>This patient's prior illness and current presentation are highly concerning for congestive heart failure caused by mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome (Kawasaki disease). Kawasaki disease is a multisystem vasculitis in children characterized by fever for at least 5 days, bilateral nonexudative conjunctivitis, a polymorphous rash, lymphadenopathy, erythema and edema of the hands and feet, and erythema affecting the mucous membranes with dry, cracked lips. To diagnose Kawasaki disease, fever for 5 days or more plus four of the remaining criteria are required. Laboratory studies may show thrombocytosis and increased ESR. Complications of Kawasaki disease include coronary artery aneurysms, and, if untreated, can lead to aneurysmal expansion, rupture, myocardial infarction, and/or congestive heart failure. Therefore, children suspected of having Kawasaki disease require immediate evaluation with ECG and echocardiography, treatment, and monitoring for progression or complications. Treatment of Kawasaki disease involves high-dose aspirin and intravenous immunoglobulin, and is the only widely accepted indication for aspirin use in febrile children due to the risk for Reye syndrome. Kawasaki disease is one of the most common pediatric vasculitides and a cause of pediatric heart disease.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Chronic inflammatory interstitial lung disease (Choice A) in childhood is rare and presents with progressive dyspnea, nonproductive cough, and tachypnea. Chest imaging will show interstitial fibrosis. The prodromal illness in this case and subsequent findings are consistent with Kawasaki disease, which does not typically involve the lungs.</p><p>Mitral valve obstruction (Choice C) would result in pulmonary edema caused by impaired forward flow of blood, which presents with progressive dyspnea, nonproductive cough, and tachypnea. Chest imaging may show congested pulmonary vasculature and increased interstitial markings. A murmur of mitral stenosis would likely be present on examination. Kawasaki disease is associated with mitral regurgitation.</p><p>Pericardial effusion (Choice D) typically presents with hypotension, tachycardia, increased jugular venous pressure, and pulsus paradoxus when severe or causing tamponade. While it is a potential complication of Kawasaki disease, the findings in this case are more consistent with congestive heart failure.</p><p>Thickened internal layer of pulmonary arterioles (Choice E) is not likely to explain this patient's symptoms, as Kawasaki disease rarely involves the pulmonary vasculature.</p>",
"19": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Influenza-induced myositis is associated with influenza A or influenza B infection and commonly presents in patients with associated viral symptoms such as fever, anorexia, and arthralgias. Myositis may occur during or after the infection, and symptoms may range from mild weakness to quadriparesis. Symptoms, laboratory findings including positive influenza testing, and increased creatinine kinase are diagnostic of this condition.</p><p>Influenza-induced myositis is a common cause of viral induced myositis and triggered by influenza A or influenza B infection. Most patients report associated viral symptoms such as fever, anorexia, and arthralgias. Myositis may occur during active infection or after the infection, and symptoms include myalgias and tenderness. Additional symptoms range from mild weakness to quadriparesis, in addition to rhabdomyolysis. Laboratory analysis may show increased creatinine kinase activity, in addition to myoglobinuria. Testing for influenza virus is diagnostic for influenza-induced myositis in the setting of appropriate signs and symptoms plus additional supporting laboratory features.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Dermatomyositis (Choice A) presents with cutaneous and musculoskeletal manifestations. Cutaneous findings include a heliotrope rash, Gottron papules overlying the joints of the dorsal hands, and a rash in a shawl distribution. Musculoskeletal findings include proximal muscle weakness in the upper and lower extremities. Diagnosis is made with a combination of clinical findings and serologic testing, including antinuclear antibody, anti-Jo-1, anti-SRP, and anti-Mi-2 antibodies. Patients diagnosed with dermatomyositis should undergo further workup including age-appropriate cancer screening to rule out an underlying occult malignancy.</p><p>Mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome (Kawasaki disease) (Choice C) is a multisystemic vasculitic disease in children characterized by fever for at least 5 days, bilateral nonexudative conjunctivitis, a polymorphous rash, lymphadenopathy, erythema and edema of the hands and feet, and erythema affecting the mucous membranes with dry, cracked lips. Complications include coronary artery aneurysms, and patients are managed with intravenous immunoglobulin and aspirin therapy.</p><p>Parvovirus B19-induced arthritis (Choice D) may be associated with acute arthritis or arthralgias in absence or presence of the classic pink patches on the bilateral cheeks of children giving an appearance of slapped cheeks. Joint symptoms are often acute, symmetric, and associated with the smaller joints in the hands, wrists, and feet. Symptoms typically resolve within 3 weeks following the diagnosis.</p><p>Rheumatic fever (Choice E) is a sequela of untreated Group A Streptococcus infection in which there is an autoimmune reaction against host proteins that have similar structure to streptococcal M protein. Its manifestations include carditis, arthritis, subcutaneous nodules, erythema marginatum, and Sydenham chorea.</p>",
"20": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Haemophilus influenzae type b is a common cause of bacterial pneumonia. A vaccine against Haemophilus influenzae type b can help prevent recurrent bacterial pneumonia in patients with T-lymphocyte immunodeficiency.</p><p>T-lymphocyte function is an essential component of host defense and is impaired in this patient, leading to recurrent bacterial infections. Haemophilus influenzae type b is an encapsulated gram-negative bacterium that can result in various mucosal infections, such as conjunctivitis, otitis media, bronchitis, and pneumonia. It was the leading cause of epiglottitis in children prior to widespread immunization. The Haemophilus influenzae type b vaccination uses conjugated capsular polysaccharide to provoke an adaptive immune response. Vaccination is a form of active immunity designed to prevent disease by stimulating the immune system to develop and maintain the ability to respond to a foreign antigen. In a patient with recurrent bacterial pneumonia, a vaccine against Haemophilus influenzae type b should be administered as it is a frequent cause of bacterial pneumonia.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>The BCG vaccine (Choice A) protects against tuberculosis, which can also cause pulmonary infections. However, it is much less common and is not routinely given to children, except in countries with a high prevalence of tuberculosis.</p><p>Rotavirus (Choice C) is a double-stranded RNA virus that causes infectious diarrhea in children. It does not cause bacterial pneumonia, thus a vaccine against Haemophilus influenzae type b is a higher priority for a patient with recurrent bacterial pneumonia.</p><p>Defense against varicella zoster virus (Choice D) relies on T-lymphocyte function, which is impaired in the setting of T-lymphocyte immunodeficiency. Infants with deficient T-lymphocyte function are at increased risk for severe infection with varicella. A vaccination against Haemophilus influenzae type b is a higher priority for a patient with recurrent bacterial pneumonia.</p><p>Administering no vaccines (Choice E) would be inappropriate, as vaccines will help protect against bacterial and viral infections in this patient with T-lymphocyte immunodeficiency.</p>",
"21": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Vaginal candidiasis involves the overgrowth of normal vaginal Candida flora. Symptoms include pruritus, erythema, and white discharge. Treatment is with topical antifungal agents such as nystatin.</p><p>This pediatric patient with vulvar erythema and white vaginal discharge likely has vulvovaginitis. Candida albicans is a fungal pathogen that causes a variety of infections, including vulvovaginitis, urethritis, thrush, and sepsis. Patients with vulvovaginitis may present with an erythematous rash, vaginal itching, and thick, white discharge. Examination is characterized by thick, white, coarse discharge, and diagnosis is via pseudohyphae on microscopy. Common risk factors include diabetes mellitus and recent antibiotic or corticosteroid use. Treatment of superficial cutaneous candidiasis infection is with topical antifungal medications such as nystatin.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Intramuscular ceftriaxone and oral azithromycin (Choice A) are antimicrobial agents used for the empiric treatment of a suspected infection with Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Chlamydia trachomatis. Vaginitis caused by these organisms is sexually transmitted, and it usually presents with mucopurulent discharge.</p><p>Oral acyclovir (Choice B) is used to treat infection caused by herpes simplex virus (HSV). Genital herpes is a sexually transmitted infection most often caused by HSV-2, and patients usually present with multiple painful vesicles on an erythematous border.</p><p>Oral amoxicillin (Choice C) combined with clavulanate can be used to treat bacterial cystitis. Patients often present with dysuria and urinary frequency. White vaginal discharge and an erythematous rash over the vulva and perineum are more consistent with fungal vulvovaginitis.</p><p>Oral metronidazole (Choice D) can be used to treat systemic fungal infections, but topical agents are preferred for superficial cutaneous infections.</p>",
"22": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Acute rhinosinusitis is usually viral in cause, and patients may present with cough, nasal congestion, clear or purulent nasal discharge, and facial pain that is worse when leaning forward. If symptoms persist beyond 10 days, a bacterial cause is more likely.</p><p>This patient with rhinorrhea, mild cough, low-grade fever, and bilateral cervical adenopathy has a presentation consistent with viral rhinosinusitis. Acute rhinosinusitis (ARS) is defined as symptomatic inflammation of the sinuses and nasal cavity lasting less than 4 weeks and can be classified as either viral or bacterial. Most cases of ARS are viral in cause, with rhinovirus, influenzavirus, and parainfluenza virus being the most implicated pathogens. Patients with viral rhinosinusitis may present with cough, nasal congestion, clear or purulent nasal discharge, and facial pain that is worse when leaning forward. In viral rhinosinusitis, fever, if present, typically resolves after 24 to 48 hours. Physical examination may show tenderness to percussion over the sinuses. Treatment of viral rhinosinusitis involves supportive care with over-the-counter analgesics, antipyretics, saline nasal spray, and oral or intranasal decongestants. Antibiotics are not indicated unless bacterial infection is suspected.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Allergic rhinitis (Choice A) commonly presents with rhinorrhea, sneezing, cough, and nasal congestion, and is often associated with allergic conjunctivitis and asthma. Chronic nasal obstruction can lead to impairment of sinus drainage, which can lead to the development of allergic sinusitis (Choice B). These conditions do not present with fever or lymphadenopathy.</p><p>Bacterial sinusitis (Choice C) has signs and symptoms that overlap those of viral rhinosinusitis, and the two can be difficult to distinguish. Bacterial sinusitis is much less common than viral sinusitis, and symptoms typically last more than 10 days. Symptoms typically present following viral upper respiratory infection, and patients may present with worsening of symptoms following an initial period of improvement. This patient with mild symptoms and fever that resolved after 2 days most likely has a viral infection.</p><p>Cervical lymphadenitis (Choice D) presents with enlarged, inflamed, and tender lymph nodes in the neck and may be either unilateral or bilateral. Bilateral cervical lymphadenitis often occurs secondary to viral upper respiratory infections, while unilateral cervical lymphadenitis is often bacterial in cause. This patient's lymph nodes are nontender.</p>",
"23": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Neurofibromatosis 1 (NF1) is an autosomal dominant phakomatosis that typically results from an inherited mutation of the NF1 tumor suppressor gene. NF1 presents with cutaneous neurofibromas, plexiform neurofibromas, hyperpigmented maculae, pigmented iris hamartomas (Lisch nodules), cognitive disability, and seizures. Patients with NF1 are at increased risk for optic nerve glioma and pheochromocytoma.</p><p>Neurofibromatosis 1 (NF1) is an autosomal dominant neurocutaneous disorder (phakomatosis) that typically results from an inherited mutation of the NF1 tumor suppressor gene on chromosome 17. NF1 is inherited with complete penetrance and variable expressivity. Rarely, the mutation in the NF1 gene occurs sporadically. NF1 presents with cutaneous neurofibromas (benign neoplasms derived from neural crest cells), plexiform neurofibromas, hyperpigmented maculae, axillary or inguinal freckling, pigmented iris hamartomas (Lisch nodules), cognitive disability, and seizures. Patients with NF1 are at increased risk for optic nerve glioma and pheochromocytoma. Optic nerve gliomas are typically indolent lesions and are most frequently low-grade pilocytic astrocytomas. The lesion may involve one or both optic nerves or the optic chiasm. Affected patients present with insidious vision loss, proptosis, and optic nerve atrophy. The diagnosis of NF1 is typically based on physical examination findings, but genetic testing may be used to confirm ambiguous cases. Treatment includes surgical debulking of tumors, management of seizures and neurological complications, and genetic counseling. The management of optic nerve glioma is controversial and may involve a combination of chemotherapy, radiotherapy, and surgical excision. Children with NF1 should undergo regular screening examinations, including ophthalmic examination, at least annually to facilitate early detection and treatment of optic nerve glioma.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Arteriovenous malformation (Choice A) is characteristic of the phakomatosis Wyburn-Mason syndrome, also known as Bonnet-Dechaume-Blanc syndrome. Wyburn- Mason syndrome presents with arteriovenous malformations of the retina and midbrain, as well as facial cutaneous angiomas.</p><p>Cerebral aneurysm (Choice B) is an important cause of intracranial hemorrhage in adults but is not associated with NF1.</p><p>Craniopharyngioma (Choice C) is a rare pituitary mass derived from remnants of the Rathke pouch that may present with bitemporal hemianopsia. NF1 is not associated with an increased risk for craniopharyngioma.</p><p>Medulloblastoma (Choice D) is the most common malignant brain tumor in children, often presenting in patients age 5 to 9 years with signs of increased intracranial pressure (eg, headache, nausea, vomiting) and gait ataxia or incoordination. NF1 is not associated with an increased risk for medulloblastoma.</p>",
"24": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Tinea corporis is a common superficial dermatophyte infection caused by direct inoculation of the skin from an affected individual or animal. Trichophyton species are a common cause of tinea corporis and diagnosis can be made clinically or with the demonstration of branching septate hyphae from skin scrapings treated with potassium hydroxide preparation. Tinea corporis is commonly treated with azole antifungals, such as clotrimazole.</p><p>Topical clotrimazole and other azole antibiotics are commonly used to treat superficial dermatophyte infections, including tinea corporis. Tinea corporis is commonly caused by Trichophyton species and results from direct inoculation from an affected individual or animal, including dogs or cats. Microscopic examination of skin scrapings with potassium hydroxide preparation shows branching septate hyphae. Tinea corporis is treated with topical azole antifungals, including clotrimazole, which inhibit conversion of lanosterol to ergosterol in the fungal cell wall by 14-α demethylase.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Topical mupirocin (Choice B) is used in the treatment of superficial antibacterial infections, such as Staphylococcus aureus. Mupirocin binds bacterial tRNA synthetase, which decreases RNA and protein synthesis. Mupirocin does not have antifungal properties.</p><p>Topical nystatin (Choice C) is a polyene that is used topically to treat mucocutaneous Candida infections. Candida infections typically present with a beefy red appearance, often in intertriginous regions, and may be associated with the development of satellite pustules. Nystatin binds cell membrane sterols, enhancing cellular permeability, and later, causing cell death to Candida species.</p><p>Oral cephalexin (Choice D) is a cephalosporin antibiotic used to prevent or treat gram-positive bacterial infections. Cephalexin does not have antifungal properties, and thus, would not be used in the treatment of tinea corporis.</p><p>Oral itraconazole (Choice E) is an azole antifungal with a similar mechanism of action to topical clotrimazole. Because of the risk for side effects, such as congestive heart failure and liver toxicity/failure, itraconazole is often reserved to treat systemic infections, such as blastomycosis, histoplasmosis, and aspergillosis. Additionally, azole antifungal agents must be used with caution, as they inhibit the cytochrome 3A4 enzyme.</p>",
"25": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Angelman syndrome is a genetic disorder caused by microdeletion of the maternally-inherited chromosome region 15q11-13, containing the gene UBE3A, and characterized by abnormal development of particular areas of the brain (eg, hippocampus, cortex, cerebellum) in which the paternally inherited copy of UBE3A is inactivated through imprinting (an epigenetic process involving selective gene inactivation through DNA and histone methylation), thus creating a reliance on the maternally inherited copy for normal development. In contrast, the paternally inherited copy of UBE3A is lost in Prader-Willi syndrome.</p><p>Angelman syndrome is a genetic disorder caused by the microdeletion of the maternally inherited chromosome region 15q11-13, which contains the gene for the ubiquitin pathway protein UBE3A. Angelman syndrome is characterized by abnormal development of particular areas of the brain (eg, hippocampus, cortex, cerebellum) in which the paternally inherited copy of UBE3A is inactivated through imprinting (an epigenetic process involving selective gene inactivation through DNA and histone methylation), thus creating a reliance on the maternally inherited copy for normal development. In contrast, microdeletion of paternally inherited UBE3A leads to the different clinical presentation of Prader-Willi syndrome. Children with Angelman syndrome present with characteristic signs and symptoms, including severe developmental delay and cognitive disability, seizures, ataxia, speech delay, and microcephaly. Patients typically also display a happy, excitable demeanor, uncontrolled laughter, and repetitive hand flapping movements. Other findings may include characteristic facial features (eg, flat posterior aspect of the head, prominent mandible with widely spaced teeth and protruding tongue) strabismus, sleep disturbances, and a notable fascination with water.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, E, and F.</b></p><p>Chromosome 7 deletion (Choice A), specifically at 7q11.23, is the cause of Williams syndrome, which presents with an atypically friendly demeanor, emotional dysregulation, intellectual delay, elfin facies, short stature, vascular and cardiac valve abnormalities secondary to elastin dysfunction, structural abnormalities of the urinary tract, and endocrine abnormalities.</p><p>Chromosome 15 deletion, paternally inherited (Choice C) is implicated in Prader-Willi syndrome, which presents with hyperphagia (and potential obesity), short stature, impaired cognition, motor delays, and hypogonadism.</p><p>Mutation of MECP2 gene (Choice D), on the X chromosome, results in Rett syndrome, which presents in females with microcephaly, cognitive and language impairment, loss of motor skills, seizures, repetitive hand wringing movements, and scoliosis. Patients may display autism spectrum disorder-like features with lack of social interest and eye contact.</p><p>Trisomy 15 (Choice E) is a rare cause of Angelman syndrome in the setting of uniparental disomy, in which paternal nondisjunction of chromosome 15 occurs, followed by maternal loss of 15q11-13. However, microdeletion of 15q11-13 without uniparental disomy is more common. Complete trisomy 15 is typically lethal in utero.</p><p>Trisomy 21 (Choice F) is the cause of Down syndrome, which presents with characteristic physical features including broad, flat, facial features with prominent epicanthal folds, and a single palmar crease. Patients with Down syndrome also present with cognitive impairment, cardiac abnormalities (eg, atrial septal defect), gastrointestinal abnormalities (eg, duodenal atresia, imperforate anus), ophthalmologic abnormalities (eg, refractive errors), and hearing loss.</p>",
"26": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Pediatric HIV/AIDS should be suspected in patients who present with recurrent bacterial pneumonia or AIDS-defining illnesses such as disseminated mycobacterial and nonmycobacterial infection, or Pneumocystis jiroveci infection.</p><p>HIV infection acquired via vertical transmission in the perinatal period is the most likely diagnosis with a history of recurrent oral thrush, otitis media, and bacterial pneumonia, as well as cervical lymphadenopathy and hepatosplenomegaly on examination. HIV transmission may occur at any time during gestation but is most acquired at or around the time of delivery and in the immediate postpartum period when the newborn is breast-feeding. Transmission is uncommon if the mother is taking antiretroviral therapy and has a low HIV viral load. As a result of universal screening for HIV in most developed countries, the incidence of perinatal and pediatric HIV has decreased, although this is not uniformly true in all countries around the world. Most patients present within the first few months of birth with recurrent pneumonia or opportunistic infections, though in some cases, symptoms and/or AIDS-defining conditions may not develop until many years later. Initial evaluation should include measurement of the HIV viral load with concomitant testing for mutations. While this patient should receive treatment for her bacterial pneumonia and thrush, she should also be started on antiretroviral therapy once HIV infection is confirmed.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, F, G, and H.</b></p><p>Chédiak-Higashi syndrome (Choice A) is caused by mutations in the lysosomal trafficking regulator gene (LYST) essential for normal formation and transportation of lysosomes within the cell. It presents with frequent bacterial infections, oculocutaneous albinism, peripheral neuropathy, and progressive neurologic dysfunction.</p><p>Chronic granulomatous disease (Choice B) is caused by a deficiency in NADPH oxidase, an enzyme used to generate hydrogen peroxide and hypochlorous acid. These molecules are typically used to kill bacteria intracellularly. Patients present with recurrent pyogenic infections with catalase-positive organisms such as Staphylococcus aureus, with normal concentrations of leukocytes and immunoglobulins.</p><p>Common variable immunodeficiency (Choice C) classically presents in the second to fourth decades of life as multiple, recurrent sinus or pulmonary infections, including pneumonia, bronchitis, and sinusitis. Testing will show low immunoglobulin concentrations.</p><p>Cystic fibrosis (Choice D) is associated with recurrent pulmonary infections as a result of the impaired clearance of mucous, microbes, and debris from the airways. Other findings include bronchiectasis, pancreatic insufficiency, diarrhea, malnutrition, and weight loss.</p><p>Severe combined immunodeficiency disease (Choice F) results from a variety of different mutations and commonly presents with recurrent bacterial, viral, and fungal infections as a result of severe impairment in both B- and T-lymphocyte function in infancy.</p><p>Terminal complement deficiency (Choice G) typically presents with recurrent infections with Neisseria species.</p><p>Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome (Choice H) is caused by a mutation in the WAS gene on the X-chromosome that encodes a protein essential for actin cytoskeleton rearrangement that occurs during interactions between T lymphocytes, antigen-presenting cells, and B lymphocytes, leading to an impaired innate and adaptive immune system. Patients present with eczema, thrombocytopenia, and infections with encapsulated bacteria and opportunistic pathogens.</p>",
"27": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Congenital hypothyroidism in infants presents with lethargy, hypothermia, feeding problems, hoarse cry, myxedematous facies, macroglossia, hypotonia, umbilical hernia, prolonged jaundice caused by impaired feeding and decreased bilirubin conjugation, and dry skin. Evaluation of thyroid function tests is necessary for early diagnosis and treatment to prevent complications and intellectual disability.</p><p>Congenital hypothyroidism typically presents without symptoms due to placental crossing of maternal thyroid hormone. However, if hypothyroidism is left untreated, infants may develop lethargy, hypothermia, feeding problems, hoarse cry, myxedematous facies, macroglossia, hypotonia, umbilical hernia, prolonged jaundice caused by impaired feeding and decreased bilirubin conjugation, and dry skin. Intellectual disability in childhood may develop. Diagnosis requires evaluation of thyroid function tests, which typically shows increased TSH and decreased thyroxine (T4) concentrations at birth. In patients with decreased thyroid hormone concentrations on serum testing, thyroid scans and thyroid-binding globulin concentrations help elucidate the cause of hypothyroidism (eg, distinguishing between thyroid dysgenesis and defective thyroid hormone synthesis). Management requires proactive newborn screening and levothyroxine therapy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</b></p><p>A karyotype analysis (Choice A) would be helpful in the diagnosis of chromosomal diseases such as Turner syndrome, congenital adrenal hyperplasia, or trisomy 21.</p><p>Measurement of sweat chloride concentration (Choice B) is used in the diagnosis of cystic fibrosis. Cystic fibrosis is a hereditary chloride channelopathy that results in thickened bronchial and gastrointestinal mucous secretions, leading to chronic infection, inflammation, and malabsorption.</p><p>CT scan of the head (Choice D) can help with the evaluation of structural brain abnormalities such as intracranial hemorrhage or congenital malformations. It has no role in the diagnosis of congenital hypothyroidism.</p><p>CT scan of the liver (Choice E) would not be helpful in the evaluation of congenital hypothyroidism. Jaundice secondary to biliary obstruction can be evaluated with ultrasonography of the right upper quadrant of the abdomen.</p>",
"28": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Salmonella enteritidis is a gram-negative bacillus that is a major cause of diarrhea worldwide. In the United States, it is most associated with undercooked poultry, eggs, or meats, and in some cases has been associated with pets (eg, turtles, reptiles, chickens). Symptoms include bloody diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, and fever. Fevers typically resolve in 2 to 3 days and diarrhea often resolves with supportive care in one week. Antibiotics are not recommended for the treatment of Salmonella enteritidis.</p><p>Salmonella enteritidis is a motile, gram-negative bacillus that is a major cause of diarrhea worldwide. In the United States, it is most associated with undercooked poultry, eggs, or meats, and in some cases has been associated with pets (eg, turtles, reptiles, chickens). The incubation period typically ranges from 8 to 72 hours and patients will subsequently develop diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, and fever. Bloody stools are common in children and Salmonella gastroenteritis is usually self-limited, with fevers resolving within 3 days and diarrhea resolving in approximately 1 week. Supportive care, including oral rehydration, is the cornerstone of management and antibiotics are not generally recommended in immunocompetent individuals with mild to moderate infections. Stool studies may show gram-negative bacilli.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Campylobacter jejuni (Choice A) is a gram-negative rod that is a common cause of bacterial diarrhea in the United States. Associated risk factors include travel and consumption of undercooked poultry or meat. Patients often present with watery diarrhea that is self-limited to 1 to 3 days. Patients are treated with supportive care and diagnosis is confirmed by stool cultures.</p><p>Escherichia coli O157:H7 (Choice B) is associated with Shiga toxin and outbreaks of bloody diarrhea. The patient's course may be complicated by hemolytic uremic syndrome, which presents with microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and kidney failure.</p><p>Listeria monocytogenes (Choice C) is an aerobic, gram-positive bacillus associated with the ingestion of unpasteurized dairy products or deli meats. The incubation period is approximately 24 hours and patients may develop associated fever, watery diarrhea, nausea, and vomiting. This is a self-limited infection, and most patients demonstrate resolution of symptoms within 2 days, however, pregnant women carry a risk for transplacental transmission to the fetus.</p><p>Shigella sonnei (Choice E) is a gram-negative bacillus associated with initial watery diarrhea, which may be followed by bloody diarrhea. Patients may also present with fevers, abdominal distention, and cramping, and diagnosis is made with a stool culture. Antimotility agents are not recommended for affected patients as they may worsen symptoms.</p>",
"29": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Gonococcal conjunctivitis is the result of inoculation from the genital canal during delivery and is associated with erythema, profuse purulent discharge, eyelid swelling, and lymphadenopathy in newborns. Newborns diagnosed with gonococcal conjunctivitis should be hospitalized and provided systemic antibiotics, such as ceftriaxone. In the hospital setting, newborns are provided erythromycin eye drops to prevent this condition.</p><p>Gonococcal conjunctivitis is the result of direct inoculation of bacteria from the genital canal to the newborn's eyes during the process of delivery. Patients present with erythema, profuse purulent discharge, eyelid swelling, and lymphadenopathy. Gram stain of purulent discharge will show gram-negative diplococci. In the hospital setting, erythromycin eye drops are provided to newborns at the time of delivery to prevent gonococcal conjunctivitis. Newborns diagnosed with gonococcal conjunctivitis should be hospitalized, cultures should be obtained, and patients should be provided ceftriaxone. Patients may also be provided artificial tears until their condition improves.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Allergic conjunctivitis (Choice A) presents with erythema, pruritus, and watery discharge involving the bilateral eyes. Patients should be instructed to avoid potential triggers, including allergens, and may be treated with topical eye drops with an antihistamine or an alpha-1 agonist.</p><p>Chemical conjunctivitis (Choice B) may be the result of chemicals meeting the conjunctiva. Symptoms including erythema, redness, pruritus or pain, and watery discharge may be present. In general, once the chemical has been washed from the eye, symptoms improve. Chlorine is a common self-limited cause of chemical conjunctivitis.</p><p>Chlamydial conjunctivitis (Choice C) typically occurs 5 to 14 days following delivery and presents with initial watery discharge that becomes mucopurulent over time. The conjunctiva may become friable and produce bloody discharge. Nucleic acid amplification tests are the gold standard for diagnosis. Treatment should only be started in infants who test positive for the condition. Azithromycin is the preferred agent of choice for neonatal chlamydial infections.</p><p>Mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome (Kawasaki disease) (Choice E) is a multisystemic inflammatory disease in children characterized by fever for at least 5 days, bilateral nonexudative conjunctivitis, a polymorphous rash, lymphadenopathy, erythema and edema of the hands and feet, and erythema affecting the mucous membranes with dry, cracked lips.</p>",
"30": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Diarrhea leads to intestinal losses of bicarbonate, leading to a non-anion gap hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis, often in association with a compensatory respiratory alkalosis.</p><p>This patient's laboratory studies and arterial blood gas analysis show a non-anion gap metabolic acidosis with partial compensation. Non-anion gap metabolic acidosis is identified by a pH less than 7.35 (acidemia), with decreased Pco2 and HCO₃⁻. The respiratory system compensates for the acidosis by increasing minute ventilation, which eliminates carbon dioxide, an acid. This results in decreased Pco2 and increased pH back toward normal range. This patient's persistently decreased pH suggests that respiratory compensation is only partial. Metabolic acidosis describes a derangement involving excess acid and is subdivided into anion gap and non-anion gap categories. Anion gap [Na⁺] - ([Cl⁻] + [HCO₃⁻]) calculates unmeasured anions in serum, which are contributing to acidity, whereas non-anion gap acidosis generally results from excess supply of protons or chloride, retention of protons or chloride by the kidney, or failure to secrete bicarbonate buffers. Non-anion gap acidosis is often a hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis, as seen in this patient. Causes of non-anion gap acidosis include infusions of normal saline, Addison disease, renal tubular acidosis, and gastrointestinal losses of bicarbonate (eg, diarrhea). In the case of diarrhea or gastrointestinal disturbance, such as in this patient, bicarbonate secreted in the small intestine is normally reclaimed in the large intestine in exchange for chloride ions. If the colon cannot reclaim bicarbonate, such as following colectomy or during diarrheal illness, the concentration of serum chloride will remain increased and serum bicarbonate decreased.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Several of this patient's laboratory findings are consistent with a presentation of metabolic acidosis secondary to diarrheal losses of bicarbonate, making a laboratory error (Choice A) unlikely.</p><p>Renal losses of bicarbonate (Choice B) occur in renal tubular acidosis. Renal tubular acidosis results in a hyperchloremic, non-anion gap metabolic acidosis from the dysregulation of the renal tubules causing the loss of bicarbonate or inability to excrete hydrogen ions. In a patient with diarrhea, stool losses of bicarbonate are more likely.</p><p>Respiratory insufficiency (Choice C) typically leads to respiratory acidosis due to hypercarbia. This patient presents with a metabolic acidosis and compensatory respiratory alkalosis, resulting in a decreased Pco2.</p><p>Vomiting (Choice E) leads to a hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis due to losses of hydrochloric acid, a major component of stomach secretions. Metabolic alkalosis also occurs secondary to dehydration and subsequent volume contraction, plus relative excess of bicarbonate.</p>",
"31": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Vitamin K is a fat-soluble vitamin that plays an important role in the synthesis of coagulation factors II, VII, IX, and X, as well as proteins C and S. Vitamin K deficiency is common in newborns due to poor placental transfer of vitamin K, low concentrations of vitamin K in breast milk, as well as immature neonatal intestinal flora and poor absorption. Vitamin K deficiency presents with bleeding (eg, bruising, petechiae, oozing from venipuncture sites, hematomas, epistaxis, or gastrointestinal, retroperitoneal, or intracranial hemorrhage). Newborns with evidence of bleeding should be treated with oral or intravenous replacement of vitamin K and fresh frozen plasma.</p><p>Vitamin K is a fat-soluble vitamin that plays an important role in the synthesis of several coagulation factors, including factors II, VII, IX, and X, as well as proteins C and S. Primary sources of vitamin K include leafy green vegetables and endogenous production by intestinal bacterial flora. Vitamin K deficiency is common in newborns due to poor placental transfer of vitamin K, low concentrations of vitamin K in breast milk, as well as immature neonatal intestinal flora and poor absorption. Vitamin K deficiency presents with signs and symptoms of bleeding (also known as hemorrhagic disease of the newborn), which may include cutaneous bleeding (eg, bruising, petechiae, oozing from venipuncture sites), hematomas, epistaxis, or gastrointestinal, retroperitoneal, or intracranial hemorrhage. All newborns should be administered prophylaxis with intramuscular vitamin K. Newborns with evidence of bleeding should be treated with oral or intravenous replacement of vitamin K and fresh frozen plasma (FFP), although treatment should be tailored according to the type and severity of bleeding. FFP contains all coagulation factors, as well as fibrinogen and proteins C and S.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</b></p><p>Cryoprecipitate (Choice A) contains concentrated fibrinogen along with factors VIII and XIII, and von Willebrand factor. It is commonly used for the treatment of coagulopathic states with decreased concentrations of fibrinogen and factor VIII, such as disseminated intravascular coagulation. Cryoprecipitate does not contain the coagulation factors that are affected by vitamin K deficiency, such as factors II, VII, IX, and X, or proteins C and S.</p><p>Factor VIII concentrate (Choice B) is useful for the treatment of bleeding disorders that are characterized by a deficiency of factor VIII, such as hemophilia A and von Willebrand disease.</p><p>Lactated Ringer solution (Choice D) is a crystalloid solution that is useful for the repletion of intravascular volume. While volume repletion may be useful in the setting of intravascular volume loss for hemorrhage, correcting the deficiency of coagulation factors by administering FFP is a more important step toward addressing this patient's underlying disease.</p><p>Platelets (Choice E) is incorrect. While vitamin K deficiency is associated with deficiency of many coagulation factors, it does not result in thrombocytopenia.</p>",
"32": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Pyloric stenosis presents at several weeks of life and is characterized by repeated nonbilious vomiting after feeds leading to dehydration and a hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis. Diagnosis is made with ultrasonography, and treatment is with surgical pyloromyotomy.</p><p>Pyloric stenosis is a disorder of the pyloric sphincter that occurs in young infants and can result in partial or complete gastric outlet obstruction. It is caused by hypertrophy of the pylorus and an inability to relax the pyloric sphincter. Infants classically present within the first 1 to 2 months of life with forceful nonbilious emesis. Physical examination is notable for a characteristic ovoid mass in the epigastric region. The recurrent emesis, if severe, may result in signs of volume depletion and malnutrition. Laboratory studies typically show a hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis as a result of the loss of gastric hydrochloric acid and potassium ions. Additionally, the resultant volume depletion causes activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, resulting in renal losses of potassium and protons. Confirmatory diagnosis is made by ultrasound imaging to evaluate the thickness and length of the pyloric muscle. Patients are treated with surgical pyloromyotomy to relieve the stenosis.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Duodenum (Choice A) is the location of obstruction in patients with duodenal atresia, which is caused by failure of recanalization of the duodenal lumen. Duodenal atresia presents with vomiting and abdominal distension within the first few days after birth in complete atresia.</p><p>Gastroesophageal junction (Choice B) is the location of obstruction in achalasia. Achalasia is an esophageal dysmotility disorder resulting from deficient peristalsis as a result of impaired neuromuscular transmission and impaired relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter. It manifests as dysphagia, odynophagia, weight loss, halitosis, and regurgitation of undigested food. It is typically diagnosed between ages 25 and 65 years and is rare in children.</p><p>Jejunum (Choice C) may undergo atresia in a fetus secondary to an ischemic event, which may have various causes, such as maternal cocaine use, malrotation with volvulus, or other idiopathic causes. Patients may present with vomiting and abdominal distension on the first day of life, similar to patients with duodenal atresia.</p><p>Midesophagus (Choice D) is the location of obstruction in patients with a vascular ring. A vascular ring is a congenital malformation of the aortic arch and/or great vessels that encircles the trachea and midesophagus. In addition to dysphagia, vomiting, and feeding problems, patients may present with stridor and respiratory difficulties.</p>",
"33": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Candida species are part of the normal skin flora. However, patients may develop infection, commonly in the diaper area or intertriginous zones. Infection presents with beefy red patches, favoring the skin folds along with satellite papules and pustules. First-line treatment includes topical antifungals, such as nystatin.</p><p>Topical nystatin is a polyene that is used topically to treat mucocutaneous candida infections. Candida is part of the normal mucocutaneous flora, but infection is common in intertriginous areas like the diaper area in infants along with the genital and inframammary regions in adults. Candida infections may be associated with beefy red patches, favoring the skin folds, with satellite papules and pustules. First-line treatment includes topical antifungals, such as nystatin.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, E, and F.</b></p><p>Topical mupirocin (Choice A) is used in the treatment of superficial antibacterial infections, such as Staphylococcus aureus. Mupirocin binds bacterial tRNA synthetase, which decreases RNA and protein synthesis. Mupirocin is not effective for the treatment of candidiasis.</p><p>Oral cephalexin (Choice C) is a first-generation cephalosporin antibiotic that is effective against gram-positive organisms as well as some gram-negative organisms. Cephalexin is not used to treat candidiasis.</p><p>Oral ketoconazole (Choice D) is an azole antifungal agent that has been associated with severe side effects including hepatotoxicity as well as QT prolongation. Because of these side effects, oral ketoconazole is rarely used in clinical practice, however, topical azoles are widely used in the treatment of cutaneous fungal infections.</p><p>Intravenous amphotericin B (Choice E) is a systemic antifungal agent that binds and disrupts ergosterol in fungal cell membranes and is used to treat systemic Candida infection. Side effects include fevers, chills, kidney toxicity, and life-threatening arrhythmias. Amphotericin B is not used for the treatment of cutaneous candidiasis.</p><p>No pharmacotherapy is indicated (Choice F) is incorrect, as a topical antifungal such as nystatin is indicated for the treatment of cutaneous candidiasis.</p>",
"34": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Part of the role of a pediatrician is to ensure the social well-being of their patients. This includes making sure that a child will be adequately cared for after the death of the parents.</p><p>In addition to caring for the physical and mental well-being of a child, part of the role of a pediatrician is to help navigate difficult social situations that may be impacting the lives of their patients. Based on his laboratory results showing a normal CD4+ count and an undetectable viral load, this patient appears to have well-controlled HIV. However, despite taking multiple antiretroviral drugs, his mother has a dangerously low CD4+ count and persistently increased viral load, and is at high risk of dying from AIDS-related complications. With a father who is not participating in this child's care, it is important for the mother to have a plan in place for who will take care of the child after her death. As a child with HIV, this patient will require lifelong antiretroviral medications, and close follow up with his pediatrician and infectious disease doctors and will need a caretaker who is aware of these needs. Although the conversation may be difficult, a pediatrician should initiate these discussions to ensure the patient will be taken care of and to offer resources to the mother if needed.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>The advantages of three-drug versus four-drug antiretroviral regimens (Choice A), the child's immunization schedule (Choice C), and long-term complications of HIV infection (Choice E) are all important things to discuss at some point. While these are important topics, the more pressing matter at this time is the care of the child after his mother’s death.</p><p>Hand-washing techniques to prevent HIV transmission (Choice D) have not been demonstrated to reduce transmission. HIV is transmitted primarily through blood, vaginal fluid, and semen, and is acquired most often through sexual intercourse and needle sharing. For this reason, handwashing is unlikely to significantly reduce transmission.</p>",
"35": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Erythema toxicum neonatorum is a common, self-limited skin eruption of newborns that begins a few days following delivery and lasts for several hours to days. This condition presents with small, yellow papules with an erythematous base in otherwise asymptomatic newborns. Parents should be provided reassurance regarding this condition.</p><p>Erythema toxicum neonatorum is a common, benign skin eruption of newborns that typically begins in the days following delivery and lasts from hours to days. This condition is characterized by 1- to 2-mm pale to yellow papules or pustules with an erythematous base, typically found on the trunk. Diagnosis is made based on clinical presentation. If performed, a bedside Wright stain of vesicular fluid would show numerous eosinophils. Reassurance that the rash will resolve in a few days following diagnosis should be provided to the newborn's parents. Erythema toxicum, in isolation, follows a benign and harmless course.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Culture of lesions and antibiotic therapy to eradicate Staphylococcus aureus (Choice A) would be appropriate in cases of superficial bacterial infections such as impetigo. Impetigo may present as a superficial erosion with an overlying honey-colored crust. Localized cases may be treated with topical antibiotics, such as mupirocin.</p><p>Culture of lesions for herpes simplex virus (HSV) (Choice B) would be the appropriate step for suspected cutaneous herpes simplex. Herpes simplex generally presents as grouped, umbilicated vesicles on the lips, genitals, or any location on the skin. In newborns, the diagnosis raises concern for disseminated HSV, for which there is a low threshold to obtain a lumbar puncture and initiate acyclovir to prevent or treat meningitis.</p><p>KOH stain of a lesion scraping for Candida species (Choice C) would be correct if the lesions were present in the genital region. Generally, candida presents in the diaper area with sharply demarcated, beefy, red plaques with satellite pustules. Candidiasis in newborns is treated with topical antifungals in the affected area.</p><p>Topical corticosteroid therapy (Choice D) is not the appropriate therapy for a self-limited condition, such as erythema toxicum neonatorum. Topical corticosteroids are commonly used in inflammatory conditions, including atopic dermatitis.</p>",
"36": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Pertussis occurs as a result of an infection from Bordetella pertussis and may lead to severe paroxysmal coughing, which may be accompanied by cyanosis and followed by syncope, emesis, or apnea. Pertussis can be prevented with immunization and is treated with antibiotics, typically macrolides.</p><p>Episodes of severe paroxysmal coughing in the setting of a respiratory illness is concerning for pertussis. Pertussis occurs as a result of an infection from Bordetella pertussis and classically presents in three stages. The catarrhal stage is first, which presents with fever, rhinorrhea, and a mild cough. This progresses to the paroxysmal stage after 1 to 2 weeks, in which patients experience violent bouts of extreme coughing, at times violent enough to cause secondary traumatic injury, such as rib fractures, urinary incontinence, and pneumothorax. Often, such coughing fits are accompanied by cyanosis and may be followed by syncope, emesis, or apnea. The paroxysmal stage can last for weeks to months. Symptoms resolve during the convalescent stage, which lasts from 1 to 4 weeks. Pertussis can be prevented with immunization in the form of acellular pertussis, typically administered along with tetanus and diphtheria vaccines. It is treated with antibiotics, typically macrolides, and supportive care.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Influenza virus (Choice B) is a common cause of viral pneumonia during the winter months. It presents with respiratory, gastrointestinal, and systemic symptoms, including cough, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, fever, chills, headache, myalgias, and arthralgias. It can be complicated by bacterial superinfection causing severe bacterial pneumonia. It typically lasts for less than 1 week and does not have a paroxysmal stage.</p><p>Mycoplasma pneumoniae (Choice C) is a common cause of atypical pneumonia and presents with acute cough, fever, chills, and shortness of breath. Chest x-ray typically shows multifocal infiltrates.</p><p>Respiratory syncytial virus bronchiolitis (Choice D) is more likely to present with fever, tachypnea, rhinorrhea, and wheezing. Patients may also display apneic episodes, although severe paroxysms of coughing would be atypical.</p><p>Streptococcus pneumoniae (Choice E) is a common bacterial cause of pneumonia, but patients tend to present with focal lung findings indicating a lobar pneumonia typically seen on chest x-ray.</p>",
"37": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: In adolescent patients, hypertension is defined as a blood pressure greater than the 95th percentile. Accurate blood pressure measurement aids in staging and in guiding treatment and blood pressure recheck intervals. If initial measurement by an oscillometric device is greater than the 90th percentile, repeat measurements by auscultation should be performed and averaged to determine the patient's stage of hypertension.</p><p>Hypertension is an increasing finding among pediatric and adolescent patients as rates of obesity have risen. Childhood and adolescent hypertension is associated with both an increased risk for persistent hypertension during adulthood and an increased risk for adverse cardiovascular events in later life. In adolescent patients, hypertension is defined as a blood pressure greater than the 95th percentile. Accurate blood pressure measurement is essential for staging and aids in guiding treatment and blood pressure recheck intervals. Oscillometric devices are commonly used for hypertension screening and routine office measurements, but frequently overestimate both systolic and diastolic blood pressures. If initial measurement by an oscillometric device reading is high, repeat measurements by auscultation should be performed and averaged to determine the patient's stage of hypertension. Initial treatment of hypertension is with lifestyle modifications targeting modifiable risk factors, such as obesity.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Echocardiography (Choice B) may be indicated in adolescent patients with hypertension in order to assess cardiac health at the time of initiating pharmacologic therapy. Establishing the diagnosis of hypertension is the more appropriate next step.</p><p>24-Hour ambulatory blood pressure monitoring (Choice C) is a useful, accurate method of assessing hypertension. However, initial, in-office measurement by auscultation should be performed first for this patient who has only undergone measurement by oscillometric device.</p><p>Repeat blood pressure measurement by oscillometric device in the office either daily for the next 3 days (Choice D) or in 2 weeks (Choice E) is incorrect. Oscillometric devices frequently overestimate blood pressure, and confirmatory measurements by auscultation are required for diagnosis.</p>",
"38": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Acute lymphoblastic leukemia is a common childhood malignancy that often presents with malaise, fatigue, weight loss, splenomegaly, lymphadenopathy, anemia, and thrombocytopenia. Diagnosis is confirmed with bone marrow biopsy showing &gt;20% lymphoblasts.</p><p>This patient, with fever, petechiae, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, and laboratory findings of pancytopenia, has a presentation concerning for acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL). ALL is a common pediatric malignancy of B- or T-lymphocytes that may present with malaise, fatigue, weight loss, splenomegaly, lymphadenopathy, anemia, and thrombocytopenia, with circulating leukemic blasts on peripheral blood smear. Diagnosis is confirmed with bone marrow biopsy showing &gt;20% lymphoblasts. B-lymphocyte malignancies are more common than T-lymphocyte malignancies, and evaluation with flow cytometry is necessary to determine the specific type of malignant cell, followed by mutational analyses. Treatment typically entails chemotherapy and many patients with ALL will enter remission. Bone marrow transplant can be curative in many cases.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Abdominal CT scan (Choice A) can be performed to identify intra-abdominal tumors such as neuroblastoma and nephroblastoma (Wilms tumors). This patient's hepatosplenomegaly is likely secondary to his leukemia rather that a primary intra-abdominal pathology.</p><p>Bone scan (Choice B) is a type of nuclear scan that uses technetium 99m methyl diphosphonate to evaluate for the presence of osteoblastic osseous metastases, most commonly in the setting of breast and prostate cancer.</p><p>Gallium scan (Choice C) is a type of nuclear scan that uses gallium-67 as the tracer to identity areas of inflammation, infection, or malignancy. This has been largely replaced by PET/CT scan for the evaluation and staging of malignancy.</p><p>Liver biopsy (Choice E) is useful in the diagnosis of conditions such as cirrhosis, hepatitis, hepatocellular carcinoma, and hepatic metastatic disease.</p>",
"39": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Thyroglossal duct cysts are common embryonic developmental abnormalities. An infected cyst typically presents with fever, dysphagia, and midline neck tenderness.</p><p>The findings of fever, chills, and midline neck pain that worsens with swallowing or sticking out the tongue is most suggestive of an infected thyroglossal duct cyst. Thyroglossal duct cysts are a common congenital abnormality that results from failure of the thyroglossal duct to close after the embryonic migration of the thyroid from where it forms in the foramen cecum to its anatomic position in the neck. The thyroid begins to form in the third week of gestation as an outgrowth from the pharynx at the junction between the anterior two-thirds and posterior third of the tongue. It then descends along the thyroglossal duct, passing anterior to the hyoid bone and stopping in the midline neck. The thyroglossal duct then closes. If a segment of the duct persists, epithelial secretions result in cyst formation.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Cat-scratch disease (Choice A) is caused by infection with the gram-negative pleomorphic coccobacillus Bartonella henselae acquired via bites or scratches from cats. It most commonly causes localized lymphadenitis along the lymphatic drainage route proximal to the scratch. The axillary and cervical lymph nodes are commonly affected. Though the patient has an exposure, his presentation is not consistent with cat-scratch disease.</p><p>Infectious mononucleosis (Choice C) is most commonly caused by infection with Epstein-Barr virus, though cytomegalovirus and acute HIV infection can also cause similar presentations. Patients typically present with lymphadenopathy (most often involving the cervical chain), pharyngitis, fatigue, and splenomegaly. This patient's examination findings are more consistent with an infected thyroglossal duct cyst.</p><p>Tonsillitis (Choice D) is a common pediatric disease caused by viral or bacterial infection. It can also present with fever, pain with swallowing, and cervical lymphadenopathy. Visualization of the tonsils demonstrating edema, erythema, and/or exudate is key to diagnosis. Tenderness in the neck over the trachea is not consistent with tonsillitis.</p><p>Viral syndromes (Choice E) are the most common cause of pediatric infections, and symptoms depend on which part of the body is affected. Upper respiratory symptoms may mimic several other conditions, which should be ruled out before attributing the illness to unspecified viral infection. Viral syndrome typically resolves over 3 to 5 days with supportive care.</p>",
"40": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome is a heterogenous genetic disorder and is characterized by above average birth weight, macrosomia, macroglossia, visceromegaly, kidney malformations, omphalocele, umbilical hernia, and an increased risk of malignancy such as Wilms tumor and hepatoblastoma. Increased serum α-fetoprotein concentrations are often seen in patients with hepatoblastoma.</p><p>Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome is a genetic disorder involving mutations of any of several genes located at the 11p15.5 locus that may result from a variety of mechanisms, including defects of imprinting or uniparental disomy. Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome is characterized by cellular overgrowth and a predisposition to the development of malignancy. Its presentation is heterogenous but commonly includes an above average birth weight, macrosomia, macroglossia, visceromegaly, kidney malformations, omphalocele, or umbilical hernia. Children with Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome are at an increased risk for developing a variety of malignancies, such as Wilms tumor and hepatoblastoma. α-Fetoprotein concentrations are markedly increased in most patients with hepatoblastoma.</p><p><b>Incorrect Choices: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Germ cell tumor (Choice A) would not present as a firm upper abdominal mass. Yolk sac tumors, a type of germ cell tumor, may present as a pelvic mass in young children and are associated with increased serum α-fetoprotein.</p><p>Nephroblastoma (Wilms tumor) (Choice C) is the most common pediatric kidney malignancy and is characterized by a large, often palpable, unilateral flank mass. Patients with Beckwith-Wiedemann are at increased risk for developing a Wilms tumor but these are not associated with increased α-fetoprotein.</p><p>Neuroblastoma (Choice D) is a malignancy of neuroendocrine cells of the sympathetic nervous system that commonly affects the adrenal glands. It may be differentiated from other common abdominal malignancies of childhood by its tendency to cross the midline and have a nodular texture, along with its association with paraneoplastic opsoclonus-myoclonus syndrome and increased concentrations of serum and urine catecholamine metabolites.</p><p>Spina bifida occulta (Choice E) is caused by the failure of caudal neuropore to close. Patients may have a tuft of hair or a skin dimple at the affected vertebral level. Increased amniotic fluid and maternal serum α-fetoprotein concentrations may be seen in other neural tube defects but is not associated with spina bifida occulta.</p>",
"41": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Common variable immunodeficiency is a primary immunodeficiency syndrome that classically presents in the second to fourth decades of life with recurrent sinus, pulmonary, and gastrointestinal infections. Laboratory studies will show low concentrations of all immunoglobulins.</p><p>The most likely diagnosis for this patient is common variable immunodeficiency (CVID). The exact cause and pathogenesis are unknown; however, the disease is characterized by combined T- and B-lymphocyte defects, dysfunction in B-lymphocyte differentiation, and a decrease in serum concentration of all immunoglobulins, as seen in this patient's laboratory studies. It commonly presents in the second to fourth decades of life with recurrent upper respiratory, pulmonary, and gastrointestinal infections. Patients may develop bronchiectasis as a result of recurrent airway infection and inflammation. CVID is also associated with persistent diarrhea as a result of chronic Giardia lamblia infection. Physical examination may show lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly. The mainstays of treatment include intravenous immunoglobulin replacement therapy and antibiotics. CVID is strongly associated with lymphoma and autoimmune conditions.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, E, and F.</b></p><p>Mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome (Kawasaki disease) (Choice B) is a multisystemic vasculitic disease resulting in acute arteritis with a risk for the development of coronary artery aneurysms. In children, the disease is characterized by fever for at least 5 days, bilateral nonexudative conjunctivitis, a polymorphous rash, lymphadenopathy, erythema and edema of the hands and feet, and erythema affecting the mucous membranes with dry, cracked lips.</p><p>Severe combined immunodeficiency (Choice C) results from a variety of different mutations and commonly presents with recurrent bacterial, viral, and fungal infections as a result of severe impairment in both B- and T-lymphocyte function in infancy. The age of this patient makes this diagnosis unlikely.</p><p>Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) (Choice D) is an autoimmune disease resulting in a constellation of symptoms, including fever, rash, photosensitivity, kidney disease, and nonerosive arthritis. The rash in SLE is typically seen over the malar prominences of the cheeks and spares the nasolabial folds.</p><p>Transient agammaglobulinemia of infancy (Choice E) is a primary immunodeficiency syndrome characterized by a drop in IgG concentrations greater than the expected physiologic hypogammaglobulinemia that naturally occurs as maternal antibodies are replaced by the infant's own during the first 6 months of life. Patients may present with recurrent bacterial infections. IgA and IgM concentrations are typically normal, and IgG returns to normal concentrations by age 6 years.</p><p>X-linked agammaglobulinemia (Choice F) is caused by mutations in the BTK gene that encodes a tyrosine kinase essential for all stages of B lymphocyte development and proliferation. Patients lack functioning B lymphocytes and therefore lack normal concentrations of immunoglobulins. It nearly always presents within the first 18 months of life with recurrent infections and is more common in males given X-linked inheritance.</p>",
"42": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Autism spectrum disorder (ASD), formerly classified as pervasive developmental disorder, includes a group of syndromes, including autism, Asperger syndrome, childhood disintegrative disorder, and pervasive developmental disorder not otherwise specified. ASD refers to a range of neurodevelopmental/neurodivergent syndromes that are characterized by atypical forms of social communication and interaction. External manifestations include avoidance of eye contact, narrow and focused interests, disinterest in typical forms of socialization and play, and a reliance on predictable routine.</p><p>Pervasive developmental disorder, which has been reclassified as autism spectrum disorder (ASD), includes a group of neurodevelopmental/neurodivergent syndromes that are characterized by atypical forms of social communication and interaction. ASD includes autism, Asperger syndrome, childhood disintegrative disorder, and pervasive developmental disorder not otherwise specified. ASD is characterized by a broad range of signs and symptoms involving restricted and repetitive patterns of behavior or activities. Children with autism often display decreased interest in socializing and may have challenging behaviors conflicting with established societal norms. Children may also display a narrow and focused range of interests, an increased reliance on routine, and apprehension to change or unexpected circumstances. The clinical manifestations of ASD typically present within the first 2 years of life with an observed delay or regression of communication and/or social skills. This typically includes decreased nonverbal communication, such as eye contact and facial expressions. Management of ASD is individualized depending on the clinical manifestations and degree of social distress for the patient. Therapy involves behavioral, developmental, social, and educational interventions to improve social functioning, communication skills, and academic functioning.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</b></p><p>Learning disorder (Choice A) is not likely for this patient. Learning disorders are characterized by a persistent impairment in reading, written expression, or mathematical ability. Patients may have difficulty with specific skills relating to these subjects. Learning disorders may coexist with ASD.</p><p>Mild intellectual developmental disorder (Choice B) refers to mild impairment related to abstract or theoretical thinking, and patients may also have decreased social and daily functional skills. They are typically able to adapt to living in society with life-skills training in specific areas.</p><p>Oppositional defiant disorder (Choice C) manifests as a persistent pattern of angry mood, defiant behavior, and vindictiveness that can cause impaired functioning in the child and distress to their caretakers.</p><p>Parent-child relational problem (Choice D) is diagnosed by the presence of pervasive dissatisfaction with the relationship, persistent thoughts of running away, and a perception that professional help is needed for maintaining the relationship. It often manifests as difficulties with conflict resolution that may escalate quickly or be marked by withdrawal that prevents resolution. There are frequent feelings of anger, contempt, apathy, and sadness.</p>",
"43": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Acute rheumatic fever is a late complication of under- or untreated Streptococcus (group A) infection characterized by carditis, subcutaneous nodules, erythema marginatum, Sydenham chorea, and migratory polyarthritis. The cause is related to immune cross-reactivity between streptococcal M protein and human cardiac myosin proteins.</p><p>The most likely diagnosis for this patient is rheumatic fever, characterized by arthritis and a murmur suggestive of mitral regurgitation. Rheumatic fever is a sequela of untreated Streptococcus (group A) infection, commonly pharyngitis, in which there is a type II hypersensitivity reaction to self-antigens having a similar structure to, and thus molecularly mimicking, streptococcal M protein. Diagnosis is made by the Jones criteria, which describe many of the core clinical features of rheumatic fever, including the major criteria of polyarthritis, carditis, subcutaneous nodules, erythema marginatum, and Sydenham chorea, as well as minor criteria of fever, leukocytosis, arthralgia, and PR interval prolongation. Patients often have an increased antistreptolysin O titer. Complications of rheumatic fever include mitral valve inflammation leading to long-term sequelae of chronic mitral valve regurgitation, prolapse, or stenosis. Treatment of the initial streptococcal infection is effective for reducing the risk for developing rheumatic fever. Per American Heart Association guidelines, once rheumatic fever has developed, treatment includes salicylates and a prolonged course of monthly penicillin treatments to reduce the risk for recurrence and further valvular damage.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, and F.</b></p><p>Dengue fever (Choice A) is caused by a flavivirus transmitted by Aedes mosquitoes in endemic tropical and subtropical regions of the world. Primary infection is typically benign, but a second exposure can result in dengue fever, which typically presents with a high fever, headache, myalgias, and arthralgias. Severe cases may be complicated by diffuse hemorrhagic disease and/or distributive shock.</p><p>Lyme disease (Choice B) is a tick-borne disease caused by the spirochete Borrelia burgdorferi and characteristically causes erythema chronicum migrans, an expanding rash that may have a central ring-like clearing. Later stages of Lyme disease can be characterized by carditis, atrioventricular heart block, facial nerve palsy (which may be bilateral), migratory arthralgias, and, if left untreated, encephalopathy and chronic arthritis.</p><p>Malaria (Choice C) typically presents with fatigue, fever, chills, and anemia in a patient with recent exposure to an endemic region, particularly within the tropical latitudes of Africa or Central/South America. Plasmodium falciparum infection can be complicated by severe disease, including cerebral malaria and acute kidney injury.</p><p>Mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome (Kawasaki disease) (Choice D) is a multisystemic vasculitis resulting in acute arteritis with a risk for the development of coronary artery aneurysms. In children, the disease is characterized by fever for at least 5 days, bilateral nonexudative conjunctivitis, a polymorphous rash, lymphadenopathy, erythema and edema of the hands and feet, and erythema affecting the mucous membranes with dry, cracked lips.</p><p>Typhoid fever (Choice F) is caused by the gram-negative bacillus Salmonella typhi and typically presents with fever, abdominal pain, and nonbloody diarrhea. Physical examination may show a characteristic rash of small red macules (called rose spots) that spreads from the trunk to the extremities.</p>",
"44": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Pyloric stenosis presents at several weeks of life and is characterized by repeated nonbilious vomiting after feedings, leading to dehydration and a hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis. Diagnosis is made with ultrasonography, and treatment is with surgical pyloromyotomy.</p><p>Pyloric stenosis is a disorder of the pyloric sphincter that occurs in young infants and can result in partial or complete gastric outlet obstruction. It is caused by hypertrophy of the pylorus and an inability to relax the pyloric sphincter. Infants classically present within the first 1 to 2 months of life with forceful, nonbilious emesis. Physical examination is notable for a characteristic ovoid mass in the epigastric region. The recurrent emesis, if severe, may result in signs of volume depletion and malnutrition. Laboratory studies typically show a hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis caused by the loss of gastric hydrochloric acid and potassium ions. Additionally, the resultant volume depletion causes activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, resulting in renal losses of potassium and protons. Confirmatory diagnosis is made with ultrasonography to evaluate the thickness and length of the pyloric muscle. Patients are treated with surgical pyloromyotomy to relieve the stenosis.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, E, and F.</b></p><p>Acute hyperammonemia (Choice A) in children is often caused by an underlying genetic metabolic abnormality (eg, ornithine transcarbamylase deficiency, medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency). Symptoms of hyperammonemia in infants include somnolence and vomiting. If severe, hyperammonemia can lead to cerebral edema.</p><p>Acute viral gastroenteritis (Choice B) often presents with vomiting and diarrhea with or without fever. This patient with progressive and forceful emesis occurring only after feeding has a presentation more consistent with gastric outlet obstruction.</p><p>Gastroesophageal reflux (Choice C) disease (GERD) is caused by acid reflux into the lower esophagus. This commonly occurs as a result of increased intra-abdominal pressure or diminished closing pressure of the lower esophageal sphincter permitting acidic contents of the stomach to reflux into the esophagus. In an infant, GERD presents with frequent regurgitation without excessive irritability.</p><p>Hemolytic-uremic syndrome (Choice D) is typically associated with infection from enterohemorrhagic Escherichia coli O157:H7 due to the production of Shiga toxin. It classically presents in children with bloody diarrhea accompanied by microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury.</p><p>Malrotation of the intestine (Choice E) results from the incomplete rotation of the midgut during embryonal development. This places the newborn at risk for midgut volvulus, which can present with bilious vomiting, abdominal distension, and pain, and, when severe, intestinal necrosis, hematochezia, perforation, and peritonitis.</p><p>Porphyria (Choice F) may present with abdominal pain, polyneuropathy, psychological symptoms, and port-wine-colored urine.</p>",
"45": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Syphilis is a spirochete that demonstrates multiple stages with varying symptoms, including primary with a painless chancre, secondary with fever, lymphadenopathy, and condylomata lata, and tertiary with tabes dorsalis, aortitis, and gummas. The painless chancre of primary syphilis can present as a painless ulcerative genital lesion.</p><p>Syphilis is a sexually transmitted disease from the spirochete Treponema pallidum that can present in multiple stages. Primarily syphilis presents with a painless chancre (ulcer) of the genitalia. There are typically no systemic signs or symptoms of syphilis infection at this stage. Rapid plasma reagin can be falsely negative in the primary stage of syphilis. Dark-field microscopy of fluid from the chancre is the most sensitive test for diagnosis of primary syphilis. The treatment for primary syphilis is a single benzathine Penicillin G intramuscular injection. Testing and treatment for additional sexually transmitted infections is appropriate, as they often coinfect patients.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, and F.</b></p><p>A gram-negative bacillus (Choice A), Haemophilus ducreyii, causes the sexually transmitted infection chancroid. Chancroid presents with painful genital ulceration and inguinal lymphadenopathy.</p><p>Gram-negative diplococcus (Choice B), such as Neisseria gonorrhoeae, can cause cervicitis, pelvic inflammatory disease, Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome, and migratory, septic arthritis. Gonococcal urethritis typically presents with dysuria and mucopurulent penile discharge.</p><p>Chlamydia trachomatis, an obligate intracellular bacterium (Choice C), is a common sexually transmitted disease that can cause cervicitis, pelvic inflammatory disease, epididymitis, and urethritis. A painless genital ulcer is more consistent with a diagnosis of syphilis.</p><p>Trichomonas vaginalis is an anaerobic, flagellated protozoan (Choice D) that causes vaginal trichomoniasis. Trichomoniasis presents with pruritus, dysuria, dyspareunia, cervical erythema, and abnormal discharge that is described as green, frothy, and malodorous. Motile trichomonads can be visualized on wet mount preparation.</p><p>A virus (Choice F) such as herpes simplex virus presents with painful genital lesions that begin as areas of erythema, erupt as clear, fluid-filled, confluent vesicles, and subsequently evolve to crusted ulcerations which heal without leaving a scar.</p>",
"46": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Orthostatic or postural proteinuria occurs commonly in adolescent males, with proteinuria occurring when upright and resolving when recumbent. A repeat urinalysis is an appropriate step in the initial workup of asymptomatic and incidentally detected proteinuria.</p><p>The first approach in assessing incidentally found proteinuria in a healthy asymptomatic child is to repeat the urinalysis. The most common cause of proteinuria in asymptomatic children is transient proteinuria secondary to other causes such as fever, stress, exercise, and dehydration. In addition, proteinuria can be orthostatic or postural, with increased protein in a standing or upright position that normalizes when a patient is supine or recumbent. Orthostatic proteinuria is common in adolescent males. A repeat urinalysis on awakening would likely show a normal urinalysis, and a urinalysis at bedtime would show proteinuria. In a patient with persistent proteinuria, further workup should be done to evaluate kidney disease.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Intravenous pyelography (Choice B) involves the intravenous injection of radio-opaque contrast to visualize the renal collecting system, ureters, and bladder under fluoroscopy. This allows for the investigation of suspected urinary tract anatomic abnormalities, malignancies, and strictures.</p><p>Renal ultrasonography (Choice C) is useful in the evaluation of obstruction, abscess, tumors, or cysts. A repeat urinalysis is a more appropriate initial step for the evaluation of potential benign proteinuria in an otherwise healthy adolescent.</p><p>A trial of corticosteroid therapy (Choice D) would be inappropriate for a benign condition such as transient or orthostatic proteinuria. This therapy may be appropriate for certain cases of nephritic or nephrotic syndrome instead.</p><p>Renal biopsy (Choice E) would be helpful in the diagnosis of kidney diseases that cause persistent proteinuria, such as nephrotic or nephritic syndromes. It would be an inappropriate next step while the diagnosis is still being established, as it is invasive and carries substantial procedural risk that may be unnecessary.</p>",
"47": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Developmental dysplasia occurs as the result of abnormal acetabular development, which leads to femoral instability. Patients may present with limb-length discrepancy and decreased abduction. Diagnosis can be made with the Barlow and Ortolani maneuvers, as well as ultrasonography. Patients with developmental dysplasia of the hip benefit from an abduction splint to prevent long-term complications, including functional disability.</p><p>Developmental dysplasia of the hip occurs as the result of an abnormal or poorly developed acetabulum in infants. Risk factors include female sex, breech position during delivery, and family history of the condition. In infants, there may be an associated limb-length discrepancy and inability to abduct the limb. In infants, the Barlow and Ortolani maneuvers may be used to test for hip instability and help diagnose developmental dysplasia of the hip. Additional testing including ultrasonography may be useful to diagnose infants younger than age 4 months. If left untreated, developmental dysplasia of the hip may result in functional disability, pain, and early osteoarthritis. The most common treatment for infants with this condition is an abduction splint, most commonly the Pavlik harness.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Acute epiphyseal fracture (Choice A) or fracture of the growth plate could occur in the case of mechanical trauma. Patients would commonly present with pain, associated ecchymosis, and guarding of the affected area.</p><p>Acute suppurative arthritis (Choice B), or septic arthritis, is the result of bacterial infection of the joint. The most common cause of septic arthritis is Staphylococcus aureus, and risk factors include hematogenous spread, direct inoculation from trauma, or may be the result of spread of infection from the overlying skin. Patients may have associated fevers, joint pain, and decreased range of motion. These patients should be treated with arthrocentesis and intravenous antibiotics.</p><p>Avulsion of muscle origin (Choice C) is commonly associated with traumatic injury, as in the case of an Achilles injury. In an avulsive injury, such as an Achilles injury, a palpable step-off may be present at the site of tendon injury or a palpable mass present in the case of a biceps injury.</p><p>Displacement of the capital femoral epiphysis (Choice D) occurs in slipped capital femoral epiphysis injury. This injury commonly occurs in adolescents and is the result of the head of the femur slipping off of the femoral neck, generally in an inferior and posterior direction. This condition is more common in young males with obesity or in a growth spurt. It can be diagnosed with frontal and frog-leg x-rays of the hips. Treatment involves conservative measures, such as crutches, or operative intervention, including surgical fixation.</p><p>Failure of osteoid to mineralize (Choice E) occurs in the case of rickets. Pediatric patients with rickets may present with bowed legs or varus deformity. Risk factors include vitamin D deficiency caused by poor dietary intake or sun absorption, a malabsorptive state, and several genetic conditions.</p>",
"48": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The US Preventive Services Task Force recommends screening all sexually active women under age 25 years for Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae infection. Early identification of these often asymptomatic infections prevents development of long-term sequelae.</p><p>For this healthy, adolescent female patient, preventive measures will primarily address her sexual health. The US Preventive Services Task Force (USPSTF) recommends screening all sexually active women under age 25 years for Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae infection. C. trachomatis is a common sexually transmitted infection that can cause various infections, including cervicitis, pelvic inflammatory disease, epididymitis, and urethritis. However, in many cases it is asymptomatic, and thus, screening asymptomatic patients is important for finding and treating active infections before long-term sequelae develop. C. trachomatis and N. gonorrhoeae commonly coinfect the same patient.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</b></p><p>Mammography (Choice A) is recommended for breast cancer screening every 1 to 2 years for patients over age 40 years.</p><p>Measurement of fasting serum glucose concentration (Choice B) and measurement of hemoglobin concentration (Choice C) are methods of screening for diabetes mellitus and anemia (in the case of routine hemoglobin testing) or diabetes mellitus (in the case of hemoglobin A1c measurement). Diabetic screening is recommended for asymptomatic adolescents who are overweight and with particular risk factors, such as evidence of insulin resistance. The USPSTF does not recommend routine screening for iron deficiency anemia for children or pregnant patients, as evidence for benefit is lacking. Screening for a sexually transmitted infection is more important for this patient.</p><p>Pap smear (Choice E) is recommended every 3 years for women aged 21 to 29 years and every 5 years in conjunction with human papillomavirus cotesting (every 3 years if cotesting is not performed) for women aged 30 to 65 years.</p>",
"49": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: There is a high prevalence of sexually transmitted infections (STIs) among adolescents and significant overlap in the clinical presentation of urinary tract infections and STIs. Patients presenting with suggestive findings, including dysuria and pyuria, should be offered diagnostic evaluation for both.</p><p>The clinician should offer testing for sexually transmitted diseases, or sexually transmitted infections (STIs), at this time. STIs including chlamydia, gonorrhea, and syphilis, are a major public health concern as they are often asymptomatic and can cause significant complications if left untreated. Adolescents are an at-risk demographic, especially as stigma regarding sexual activity and concerns about confidentiality may make individuals uncomfortable in disclosing any exposures or concerns regarding STIs. Urinary symptoms are a common presenting complaint for sexually active patients, and urinary tract infections (UTIs) often occur concurrently with STIs. Patients presenting with clinical findings of infection, such as dysuria and pyuria in this case, should be evaluated for both urinary tract infections and sexually transmitted infections, and appropriate treatment based on the results should be prescribed.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Sitz baths (Choice A) can help alleviate symptoms of a UTI but should not replace appropriate diagnostic testing and antimicrobial therapy. Sitz baths are more commonly used for perianal pathologies, such as hemorrhoids.</p><p>Wet mount preparation with KOH (Choice C) is useful in diagnosing Candida vulvovaginitis, which typically presents with pain, pruritis, dysuria, and dyspareunia. Examination typically shows vulvar and/or vaginal erythema and thick, white discharge. This patient's clinical findings are more consistent with UTI or STI.</p><p>X-ray screening for urinary anomaly (Choice D) is not indicated at this time. Patients presenting with recurrent urinary tract infections may have an underlying anatomic abnormality causing obstruction of the flow of urine and stasis predisposing them to infection. Additionally, ultrasound, CT imaging, or fluoroscopic studies are generally more sensitive for detecting structural urinary anomalies than a plain film x-ray.</p><p>No intervention (Choice E) is not appropriate as untreated STIs may have severe complications including upper genitourinary tract infection, pelvic inflammatory disease, abscess, and infertility.</p>",
"50": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Gastroesophageal reflux disease is extremely common in healthy infants because of diminished closing pressure of the lower esophageal sphincter. Infants who demonstrate good weight gain, are feeding well, and not irritable, are managed conservatively with lifestyle interventions. Infants experiencing weight loss should undergo nutritional workup, may undergo ultrasound testing, and start a trial of proton pump inhibitors to prevent worsening. Formula-fed children may require thickened feeds or transition to breast milk for improvement of their condition.</p><p>Gastroesophageal reflux disease is extremely common in infants. This is the result of diminished closing pressure of the lower esophageal sphincter, permitting acidic contents of the stomach to reflux into the esophagus. Reflux events may occur up to 30 times per day. Some of these episodes result in contents entering the oral cavity and decrease through the first year of life. Infants who demonstrate good weight gain, are feeding well, and are not irritable, are managed conservatively. Modifications that may improve symptoms include having infants avoid secondhand smoke exposure, smaller and more frequent meals, and keeping infants upright after feeding when possible. Infants who experience reflux and poor weight gain should undergo further evaluation for calorie count, ultrasonography to rule out pyloric stenosis, and have their diets adjusted to thickened feeds or breast milk. In patients with refractory cases, a trial of proton pump inhibitors may be warranted.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</b></p><p>Inflammatory bowel disease (Choice B) includes ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease. Ulcerative colitis is associated with inflammation of the mucosal layer of the colon and manifests with bloody diarrhea, abdominal pain, and tenesmus. Crohn disease may occur at any point in the gastrointestinal tract and symptoms differ based on the area of involvement.</p><p>Necrotizing enterocolitis (Choice C) is associated with intestinal necrosis. This condition is most common in premature infants and is diagnosed by history, examination, and imaging (pneumatosis intestinalis on x-ray). Treatments include antibiotics and surgical intervention in the case of intestinal perforation.</p><p>Pancreatitis (Choice D) is associated with the development of abdominal pain, often radiating to the back, nausea, vomiting, fevers, and epigastric tenderness. Laboratory analysis may show increased amylase and lipase, while imaging may show peripancreatic inflammation. This patient's presentation is not consistent with acute pancreatitis.</p><p>Pyloric stenosis (Choice E) is caused by hypertrophy of the pyloric sphincter, resulting in gastric outlet obstruction, which typically first presents at around 3 to 6 weeks of life. It is characterized by repeated vomiting after feeds, leading to dehydration and a hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis.</p>"
};

